&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


SteamVRの映像品質は更新される。では「眼そのもの」の見え方は？
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;今週、Road to VRが報じたのは、ValveによるSteamVRベータアップデートの話でした。内容はシンプルで、Steam Linkを通じてワイヤレスでPCのSteamライブラリを楽しめるスタンドアロンヘッドセット——Meta Quest、HTC Vive Focus、PICOのコンシューマー向けモデル——に対して、ストリーミング映像品質が改善されたというものです（出典: &lt;a href="https://roadtovr.com/steamvr-beta-update-image-quality-quest-frame-pico/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://roadtovr.com/steamvr-beta-update-image-quality-quest-frame-pico/&lt;/a&gt; ）。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;派手なニュースではありません。しかし、この一件はVRというプラットフォームの性格をよく表しています。&lt;strong&gt;ハードウェアの見え方は、購入後にソフトウェアで更新されうる。&lt;/strong&gt; 解像度やフレームレートの体感は、ファームウェアとアルゴリズムの改善によって、後から静かに良くなっていく。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


レンダリングパイプラインの話として読む
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;エンジニアの目で見ると、このアップデートは「帯域とレイテンシのトレードオフをどう詰めるか」という、ストリーミングXRの古典的な課題に対する一つの回答です。QuestやPICOのようなスタンドアロン機は、PCのGPUリソースを直接使えません。映像はエンコードされ、Wi-Fi経由で送られ、ヘッドセット側でデコードされます。ここで効いてくるのが、圧縮レート、フレームペーシング、そして再投影（reprojection）の精度です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;興味深いのは、この種の改善が「ハードを買い替えずに体感を上げる」方向に最適化されている点です。つまり、&lt;strong&gt;知覚品質（perceived quality）はソフトウェアスタックの関数&lt;/strong&gt;だということ。解像度という物理量ではなく、遅延・圧縮・補正の組み合わせが、ユーザーの「きれいになった」を作る。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


では、「眼そのもの」の見え方はどうか
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここで一度、立ち止まって考えたいことがあります。ヘッドセットの映像品質はベータアップデートで改善されます。では、私たちの両眼が協調して一つの立体像を作る能力——つまり&lt;strong&gt;目の使い方&lt;/strong&gt;は、誰かが更新してくれるのでしょうか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;答えは、ノーです。とくに弱視（lazy eye、amblyopia）や両眼視機能の協調に課題がある場合、その改善は「自分で訓練する」という能動的な行為になります。ここが、ハードウェアの更新とヒトの視覚訓練の決定的な違いです。前者はプッシュ型、後者はプル型。ベンダーが配信してくれるものではなく、本人が時間を投じるしかない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


私の場合、それは父との夕食の時間だった
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;少しだけ、個人的な話をさせてください。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;三年前、父が還暦を過ぎた頃、食卓でこんなことを言いました。「右目で、お前の顔がぼやけるんだよ」。冗談だと思った。でも、その数週間後、病院の検査用チャートを前にした父は、右目の列の下のほうが読めず、黙り込んでしまいました。医師は「amblyopia（弱視）の要素がある」と説明し、アイパッチや訓練の話をしました。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;父は、パッチを貼った。最初の一週間は頑張った。二週間目、テレビの前に座って、貼ったパッチの上から「つまらない」と呟いた。あの声を、私はよく覚えています。夕食のカレーの匂いと、リビングの照明の白さと、父の諦めたような小さなため息。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;訓練は、続かなければ意味がない。でも、続かないものを、人は「意志が弱い」と言う。私はそれが、ずっと引っかかっていました。エンジニアとして、僕はこう考えていました——&lt;strong&gt;継続できないのは、ユーザーの問題ではなく、設計の問題だ。&lt;/strong&gt; 毎日同じ退屈なタスクを強いるUIは、どんなに臨床的に正しくても、リテンションで負ける。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


システム設計としての「訓練」
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;転機は、私がVRヘッドセットを買ったことでした。SteamVRのニュースを追いながら、ふと、弱視向けのVRアプリがあることを知りました。それが、Seven Sportsが開発した&lt;strong&gt;Amblyotube&lt;/strong&gt;です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;発想を、少し技術的に分解してみます。VRヘッドセットには独立した二つの接眼レンズがある。つまり、&lt;strong&gt;左右の眼に対して独立したレンダリングパスと独立した視覚フィードを持てる。&lt;/strong&gt; これは両眼視の訓練にとって本質的で、モニターやスマホでは原理的に再現できない。左右に別々の画像を、フレーム同期を保ったまま提示できるのは、ヘッドセットというハードウェアの構造そのものが持つ強みです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そのうえで、&lt;strong&gt;YouTubeの動画をそのまま教材にしてしまう。&lt;/strong&gt; ここが設計上のキモでした。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;起動時に、影響を受けている眼（左／右）と重症度プリセット（軽度／中等度／重度）を選ぶ。間違えると優位眼を訓練してしまうので、ここは慎重に。設定は状態として保持され、以降のセッションに引き継がれます。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;内蔵の&lt;strong&gt;LogMAR視力チェック&lt;/strong&gt;（Landolt C視標、仮想3メートル、最大30試行、臨床的staircaseアルゴリズム）を使えば、左右それぞれと両眼のlogMARスコアが出て、&lt;strong&gt;重症度プロファイルを自動更新&lt;/strong&gt;できる。つまり、訓練の入力パラメータを、測定という客観的なシグナルでキャリブレーションできる。手動の勘に頼らない。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;動画はバーチャルルームでも、&lt;strong&gt;Room View（ARパススルー）&lt;/strong&gt;で現実の部屋に重ねても再生できる。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;優位眼には&lt;strong&gt;Dominant Eye Shader&lt;/strong&gt;（blur、contrast、brightness、gamma、opacityを調整可能。opacity 100%で完全なデジタルパッチ）をかける。物理パッチの代替を、シェーダのパラメータとして表現しているわけです。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;弱視眼には&lt;strong&gt;Lazy Eye Sharpener（MFBFモード）&lt;/strong&gt;。AIが人物（顔・体）を検出し、その眼の映像だけを選択的にシャープにする。さらに赤い輪郭（Outline）や黄緑のハイライト（Highlight）で人物を強調し、&lt;strong&gt;Pulse Hz&lt;/strong&gt;と&lt;strong&gt;Pulse depth&lt;/strong&gt;で点滅のリズムを調整できる。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;自然な動画背景に対して目立つ&lt;strong&gt;マゼンタのフォーカスキュー&lt;/strong&gt;を弱視眼に、控えめなグレーを優位眼に提示し、脳に二つの信号を統合させる——&lt;strong&gt;両眼融合（binocular fusion）と奥行き知覚&lt;/strong&gt;の練習。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Wedge Occlusion&lt;/strong&gt;で左右の眼が別々の領域を見るようにし、二眼の競合を減らす。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;面白いのは、これらがすべて「既存のコンテンツを加工する」レイヤーとして実装されている点です。新しい映像素材を用意する必要はない。&lt;strong&gt;コンテンツと訓練ロジックを分離した&lt;/strong&gt;からこそ、父が見たい動画が、そのまま視覚のジムになる。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


30〜40分、好きなコンテンツで
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;推奨は1日30〜40分（最大1時間）、対象年齢は13歳以上。コントローラーでもハンドトラッキングでも操作でき、子向けのオフラインカタログも用意されています。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;父に試してもらった初日、彼はいつも見ている時代劇を選びました。十分ほどで「右の目で、人物の輪郭が赤く光っとる」と言い、二十分ほどで「あ、奥行きが出てきた気がする」と、少し笑いました。あの「つまらない」と同じ口から出た言葉とは、思えませんでした。毎日続いたのは、意志が強くなったからじゃない。見たいものが、そのまま訓練になったからです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;重要なのは、これは&lt;strong&gt;補助的な視覚トレーニング／支援ツール&lt;/strong&gt;であり、両眼の協調と奥行き知覚を扱うものだという点です。診断・治療・治癒を目的とした医療機器ではありません。気になる症状があれば、まず眼科の専門家に相談してください。父も、通院は続けています。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


まとめ
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SteamVRのベータ更新は、VR体験の「器」が継続的に良くなっていくことを教えてくれました。レンダリングも、圧縮も、再投影も、誰かがアップデートしてくれる。Amblyotubeが扱うのは、その器の中で起きる「あなた自身の見え方」です。器は誰かが更新してくれる。見え方は、自分で訓練する。続けるコツは、意志の強さではなく、&lt;strong&gt;好きな動画をそのまま使えること&lt;/strong&gt;なのだと思います。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その区別に納得できたなら、次の一歩はここからです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Meta QuestアプリストアでAmblyotubeをダウンロード:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.meta.com/en-gb/experiences/amblyotube/25906906972338493/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.meta.com/en-gb/experiences/amblyotube/25906906972338493/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;本記事は視覚に関する一般的な情報提供を目的としており、医学的助言ではありません。Amblyotubeは補助的な視覚トレーニング／支援ツールであり、専門的な医療に代わるものではありません。&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tencent가 11월 7일 HF에 올린 &lt;code&gt;Qwen3.8-27B-DSpine-b16&lt;/code&gt;은 Qwen3.8-27B의 속도를 높이기 위한 &lt;strong&gt;투기 디코딩 초안 모델&lt;/strong&gt;입니다. 초안 모델이 한 번에 16토큰을 병렬로 제안하고, 큰 모델이 그것을 한꺼번에 검증하는 방식입니다. 카드에는 비교 대상인 DFlash2보다 수락 길이가 길다는 표와, 자기회귀 디코딩 대비 최대 3.84배라는 처리량 표가 있습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;저희는 이 체크포인트를 실행하지 않았습니다. 대신 카드의 에이전트 수치 10칸을 가져다 계산했고, 가중치 파일은 받지 않고 safetensors 헤더만 HTTP Range로 읽었습니다. 확인된 것은 네 가지입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;수락 길이는 DFlash2 대비 &lt;strong&gt;평균 22.8%&lt;/strong&gt; 늘었지만(최소 15.4%, 최대 32.8%), 처리량은 &lt;strong&gt;평균 14.0%&lt;/strong&gt; 늘었습니다(최소 8.2%, 최대 21.2%). 열 칸 모두에서 처리량 증가율이 더 작았습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;초안 모델은 BF16으로 &lt;strong&gt;20억 2,591만 개 파라미터(4.05GB)&lt;/strong&gt;이고, 대상 모델 27.78B의 7.3%입니다. DFlash2(19억 2,440만 개)보다 5.3% 큽니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;일반 벤치마크에서 DSpine의 수락 길이 이득은 탐욕 디코딩(T=0) 평균 18.6%에서 샘플링(T=1) 평균 9.6%로 줄었습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;이 수치는 전부 저자 보고이며 저희가 재현한 값이 아닙니다. 계산한 값은 "계산"이라고 표시했습니다. 비드래프트는 이 모델의 제작에 관여하지 않았습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-6cd426e5-a9fc-4263-9259-e8bc9fa3e158" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-6cd426e5-a9fc-4263-9259-e8bc9fa3e158" alt="DSpine 대 DFlash2, 수락 길이 증가율과 처리량 증가율" width="2000" height="840"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


수락 길이가 늘면 처리량도 그만큼 늘까요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;투기 디코딩은 작은 초안 모델이 토큰 여러 개를 먼저 써 보고, 큰 모델이 한 번의 순전파로 그 토큰들을 검증해서 맞는 만큼만 채택하는 방식입니다. &lt;strong&gt;수락 길이&lt;/strong&gt;는 한 번의 초안-검증 주기에서 채택된 토큰의 평균 개수입니다. 수락 길이가 5라면 큰 모델을 한 번 돌려 평균 5토큰을 얻는 셈입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;그래서 수락 길이가 속도의 지표처럼 쓰이지만, 속도 그 자체는 아닙니다. 주기 하나가 걸리는 시간이 같이 변하기 때문입니다. 초안 블록이 8토큰에서 16토큰으로 커지면 검증해야 할 토큰이 늘고, 초안 모델이 커지면 초안 단계가 길어집니다. 또 카드의 처리량은 "총 출력 토큰을 동시 재생 전체 시간으로 나눈 값"이라서, 가속되지 않는 시간(프리필, 스케줄링 등)도 분모에 들어갑니다. 수락 길이가 두 배가 되어도 처리량이 두 배가 되지 않는 이유입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;그러면 카드의 수치로 얼마나 벌어지는지 직접 계산해 보겠습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


DSpine 파일 안에는 무엇이 들어 있나요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;모델 카드는 구조를 한 줄로 설명합니다. 각 층이 예측한 이전 위치의 특징을 옆 위치로 넘겨서, 깊이 방향으로 인과 의존을 전달한다는 내용입니다. 파일 쪽에서는 &lt;code&gt;config.json&lt;/code&gt;과 safetensors 헤더로 확인되는 것만 적겠습니다. 헤더는 파일 맨 앞 8바이트가 JSON 길이이므로, 가중치 본체를 받지 않고 몇 KB만 읽으면 됩니다.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;head&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;repo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://huggingface.co/{repo}/resolve/main/model.safetensors"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;urlopen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Range"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bytes={a}-{b}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unpack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&amp;lt;Q"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 앞 8바이트 = JSON 헤더 길이&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;               &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 가중치 본체는 받지 않는다&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;head&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tencent/Qwen3.8-27B-DSpine-b16"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"__metadata__"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;prod&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;__import__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"math"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;prod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;bf16&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;prod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shape"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;values&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;v&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dtype"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"BF16"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"BF16 파라미터 {bf16:,}개, {bf16 * 2 / 1e9:.2f} GB"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"embed/lm_head 텐서:"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"embed"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lm_head"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;실행하면 다음과 같이 나옵니다.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;BF16 파라미터 2,025,912,064개, 4.05 GB
embed/lm_head 텐서: []
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;헤더에서 읽은 구성은 이렇습니다. 합은 20억 2,591만 개이고, 정수형 버전 표시 스칼라 4개는 뺐습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;덩어리&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;파라미터&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;비중&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MLP (5층)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;13억 3,693만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;66.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;어텐션 q/k/v/o (5층)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2억 6,214만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12.9%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;selected_prefix_bridge.codebook&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2억 5,428만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12.6%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;fc&lt;/code&gt; (5개 층 특징을 합치는 투영)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1억 3,107만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;나머지 (브리지의 다른 가중치, 정규화)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4,148만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;몇 가지를 읽을 수 있습니다. 먼저 초안 모델에는 &lt;strong&gt;임베딩과 출력층 텐서가 없습니다.&lt;/strong&gt; 어휘 크기가 248,320이므로 따로 가지면 12억 7천만 개씩일 텐데, 파일에는 없습니다. 대상 모델의 것을 빌려 쓰는 구조로 보이지만, 이것은 헤더에서 추정한 것이고 코드를 읽어 확인한 것은 아닙니다. 다음으로 &lt;code&gt;fc&lt;/code&gt;의 모양이 &lt;code&gt;[5120, 25600]&lt;/code&gt;인데, 25,600은 5,120의 정확히 5배이고 &lt;code&gt;config.json&lt;/code&gt;의 &lt;code&gt;target_layer_ids&lt;/code&gt;가 다섯 층(1, 16, 31, 46, 61)입니다. 대상 모델의 다섯 층에서 뽑은 은닉 상태를 이어 붙여 5,120차원으로 줄이는 투영으로 읽는 것이 자연스럽습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;codebook&lt;/code&gt;은 모양이 &lt;code&gt;[248320, 1024]&lt;/code&gt;입니다. 어휘 토큰마다 1,024차원 벡터를 가진 표로 보이고, 초안 파라미터의 12.6%를 차지합니다. 이름과 모양 말고는 확인한 것이 없으므로 역할은 논문(arXiv 2609.36173)과 저장소 코드를 보셔야 합니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;대상 모델 쪽은 &lt;code&gt;Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B&lt;/code&gt;의 &lt;code&gt;config.json&lt;/code&gt;에서 읽었습니다. 64층 중 16층이 전체 어텐션이고 나머지 48층이 선형 어텐션이며, 은닉 크기 5,120과 MLP 중간 크기 17,408은 초안과 똑같습니다. HF 메타데이터의 총 파라미터는 277억 8,143만 개입니다. 이 저장소는 비전 설정이 함께 들어 있는 멀티모달 구조라서 텍스트 부분만의 값은 이보다 작습니다. 비교는 다음과 같습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;모델&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;파라미터&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;대상 대비&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Qwen3.8-27B (대상)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;277억 8,143만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DSpine-b16 (초안)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20억 2,591만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7.3%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DFlash2 (초안, &lt;code&gt;z-lab&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;19억 2,440만&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6.9%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DSpine은 DFlash2보다 약 1억 150만 개(5.3%) 큽니다. 초안 블록은 16토큰으로 DFlash2의 8토큰보다 길고, 카드는 동적 검증과 ReplaySSM을 켰다고 적었습니다. 크기와 블록 길이와 추론 설정이 동시에 다르므로, 어느 요소가 이득을 만들었는지는 카드의 표로는 가를 수 없습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


카드 수치 열 칸을 계산하면 무엇이 보이나요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;카드의 에이전트 표는 수락 길이와 처리량을 각각 DFlash2와 DSpine으로 줍니다. 처리량 표에는 자기회귀(AR) 대비 배수도 괄호로 적혀 있습니다. 여기서 한 가지를 더 계산했습니다. 속도 배수 S가 수락 길이 L을 주기 비용 c로 나눈 값이라고 보면 &lt;code&gt;c = L / S&lt;/code&gt;입니다. c는 "초안-검증 한 주기가 자기회귀 1토큰 디코딩의 몇 배 시간이 걸리나"를 나타내는 눈금입니다. 이것은 &lt;strong&gt;저희가 카드 수치에서 유도한 값이지 측정값이 아닙니다.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;S&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;5.63&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;805.53&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;210.03&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# WorkBuddy Office (low): DSpine 수락 길이, 처리량/AR&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;L&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;S&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;           &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 1.47: 한 주기가 AR 1토큰의 약 1.47배 시간&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;열 칸 전체의 결과입니다. 수락 길이와 처리량은 카드 값이고, 증가율과 c는 계산입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;과제 (추론 강도)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;수락 길이 DFlash2에서 DSpine&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;수락 길이 증가&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;처리량 증가&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;암시 주기 비용 c&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Code (low)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.19에서 5.08&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+21.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+16.3%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.80에서 1.88&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Code (xhigh)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.06에서 4.92&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+21.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+11.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.42에서 1.55&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Web (low)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.95에서 4.91&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+24.3%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+20.9%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.53에서 1.57&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Web (xhigh)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.95에서 5.01&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+26.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+14.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.36에서 1.50&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Office (low)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.24에서 5.63&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+32.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+21.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.34에서 1.47&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WorkBuddy Office (xhigh)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.20에서 5.49&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+30.7%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+15.3%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.36에서 1.55&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SWE-bench Pro (low)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.19에서 4.99&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+19.1%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+8.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.54에서 1.69&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SWE-bench Pro (xhigh)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.00에서 4.77&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+19.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+11.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.39에서 1.50&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;τ²-bench (low)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.70에서 5.53&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+17.7%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+8.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.59에서 1.73&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;τ²-bench (xhigh)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4.29에서 4.95&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+15.4%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+12.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.67에서 1.72&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-81d54d8d-5794-42cc-be89-9afa1777fccf" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-81d54d8d-5794-42cc-be89-9afa1777fccf" alt="초안-검증 한 주기의 암시 비용" width="1600" height="840"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;세 가지가 보입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;열 칸 모두에서 DSpine이 앞섭니다.&lt;/strong&gt; 처리량 증가율이 가장 낮은 칸도 +8.2%입니다. 카드가 말하는 "DFlash2보다 빠르다"는 이 표 안에서는 일관됩니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;그러나 수락 길이 이득이 처리량으로 전부 옮겨 가지는 않았습니다.&lt;/strong&gt; 평균 22.8% 대 14.0%이고, 암시 주기 비용은 DSpine 쪽이 2.8%에서 13.3%(평균 7.8%) 높습니다. 블록 16토큰 검증, 5.3% 큰 초안 모델, 동적 검증 비용이 여기에 들어 있을 것으로 짐작하지만, 카드에는 이를 분리한 실험이 없습니다. 게다가 앞서 말한 가속되지 않는 시간이 분모에 들어가서, 비용이 전혀 늘지 않았더라도 처리량 증가율은 수락 길이 증가율보다 작아집니다. 두 효과를 이 표로는 나눌 수 없습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DFlash2의 c 자체가 과제마다 1.34에서 1.80으로 흩어집니다.&lt;/strong&gt; 주기 비용이 모델과 하드웨어로만 정해진다면 이렇게 퍼지지 않을 것입니다. 과제별 프리필 비중과 도구 호출 재생 시간이 처리량에 섞여 있다는 뜻으로 읽힙니다. c는 판정이 아니라 눈금으로만 쓰시길 권합니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;한 가지를 더 짚어 둡니다. 카드에 따르면 WorkBuddy 세 부분집합은 CodeBuddy 샌드박스에서 기록한 궤적의 도구 호출 앞부분을 순서대로 재생하며 얻은 값이고, 부분집합당 대화는 20개입니다. 실제 에이전트가 도구 결과를 받으며 진행하는 환경과는 다를 수 있고, 표본이 크지 않으므로 소수점 둘째 자리의 차이를 해석하는 것은 무리입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


온도를 올리면 이득이 얼마나 줄어드나요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;일반 벤치마크 표는 탐욕(T=0)과 샘플링(T=1, top_p 0.95, top_k 20)을 나란히 줍니다. DFlash2 대비 DSpine의 수락 길이 증가율을 계산하면 다섯 과제 모두에서 T=1이 작습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;과제&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;T=0 증가&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;T=1 증가&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GSM8K&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+26.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+15.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MATH-500&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+18.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+7.4%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HumanEval&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+17.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+8.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MBPP&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+21.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+12.9%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MT-Bench&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+9.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+3.6%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;평균&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+18.6%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;+9.6%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-77c4fc6d-e7f8-472c-917c-2809df4ad959" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fquickchart.io%2Fchart%2Frender%2Fzf-77c4fc6d-e7f8-472c-917c-2809df4ad959" alt="온도에 따른 수락 길이 증가율" width="1600" height="840"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;샘플링을 하면 큰 모델이 고르는 토큰이 초안과 어긋날 확률이 올라가므로 수락 길이가 줄어드는 것은 자연스럽습니다. 눈여겨볼 점은 줄어드는 폭이 DFlash2보다 DSpine 쪽에서 더 크다는 것입니다. 병렬로 16토큰을 제안하는 만큼 뒤쪽 위치의 예측이 샘플링 잡음에 더 민감한 것으로 보이는데, 이것은 숫자에서 읽은 추측이고 원인을 확인한 것은 아닙니다. 서비스가 낮은 온도로 도는지 높은 온도로 도는지에 따라 기대 이득이 크게 달라질 수 있다는 점은 분명합니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


초안 모델이 메모리를 얼마나 더 먹나요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;가중치는 앞서 계산한 대로 BF16 4.05GB입니다. KV 캐시는 &lt;code&gt;config.json&lt;/code&gt;에서 계산할 수 있습니다. 초안은 5층, KV 헤드 8개, 헤드 차원 128이고 슬라이딩 윈도우가 8,192입니다. 대상 모델은 전체 어텐션 16층, KV 헤드 4개, 헤드 차원 256입니다. 선형 어텐션 48층은 길이에 상관없이 일정한 크기의 상태를 가지므로 이 계산에서 뺐습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;항목&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;토큰당 KV (BF16)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;컨텍스트 131,072일 때&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;대상 모델 (전체 어텐션 16층)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;65,536B&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8.0 GiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DSpine 초안 (5층)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20,480B&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;창 8,192로 상한 160 MiB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;초안의 토큰당 KV는 대상의 31%이지만, 윈도우가 있어서 컨텍스트가 길어져도 요청당 160 MiB에서 멈춥니다. 카드의 서버 설정(&lt;code&gt;--max-running-requests 8&lt;/code&gt;)대로 8개가 동시에 돌면 초안 KV는 최대 1.25 GiB입니다. 이것은 BF16 KV를 가정한 이론값입니다. 실제 SGLang이 확보하는 양은 페이지 단위 할당과 mamba 캐시 설정(&lt;code&gt;--max-mamba-cache-size 64&lt;/code&gt;) 때문에 다를 수 있으므로, 올리기 전에 직접 재야 합니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


내 서버에 올리기 전에 무엇을 재야 하나요?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;카드의 서버 명령에는 &lt;code&gt;--enable-linear-replayssm-spec&lt;/code&gt;이 들어 있습니다. 일반론으로 설명하면, 선형 어텐션 층의 상태는 토큰마다 덮어써지므로 초안이 틀렸을 때 KV처럼 뒤쪽만 잘라 버릴 수가 없고, 되감는 장치가 필요합니다. 이 옵션이 그 역할이라고 짐작하지만 구현은 확인하지 못했습니다. 중요한 것은 이 모델을 쓰려면 일반 SGLang이 아니라 DSpine 저장소를 설치하고 &lt;code&gt;--speculative-algorithm DSPINE&lt;/code&gt;을 써야 한다는 점입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;도입 전에 점검할 것을 정리하면 이렇습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;동시 요청 수를 맞춰 AR 대비로 재십시오.&lt;/strong&gt; 카드의 모든 수치는 동시성 8입니다. 일반적으로 동시 요청이 많아져 GPU가 연산 병목에 가까워지면 투기 디코딩의 이득은 줄어드는 경향이 있습니다. 카드의 3배가 내 서버의 3배라는 보장은 없습니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;내 요청 분포로 수락 길이를 기록하십시오.&lt;/strong&gt; 온도, 도구 호출 비중, 출력 길이에 따라 수락 길이가 달라집니다. 이 글의 표는 카드가 고른 과제에서의 값입니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;메모리를 따로 잡으십시오.&lt;/strong&gt; 초안 가중치 4.05GB에 요청당 KV 160 MiB 이하를 더하고, 선형 어텐션 상태 여유를 따로 확인하십시오.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;출력이 달라지지 않는지 확인하십시오.&lt;/strong&gt; 투기 디코딩은 설계상 검증을 통과한 토큰만 채택하므로 탐욕 디코딩 결과가 AR과 같아야 합니다. 다만 수치 비결정성으로 일부가 갈릴 수 있으니 고정 입력 몇십 개로 비교해 보십시오.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


자주 묻는 질문
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;수락 길이가 길면 항상 더 빠른가요?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
아닙니다. 속도는 수락 길이를 주기 비용으로 나눈 값에 가깝고, 가속되지 않는 시간도 섞입니다. 이 글의 계산에서도 수락 길이는 평균 22.8% 늘었지만 처리량은 14.0%만 늘었습니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;카드의 최대 3.84배는 어디서 나온 숫자인가요?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
WorkBuddy Office(low)에서 자기회귀 210.03 tokens/s 대비 DSpine 805.53 tokens/s로 계산한 값입니다. 동시성 8, BF16, FlashAttention 3, SGLang 0.5.22 조건이고 저자 보고입니다. 같은 표에서 SWE-bench Pro(low)는 2.95배이므로 과제에 따라 폭이 큽니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DSpine이 DFlash2보다 낫다고 결론 내려도 되나요?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
카드 안의 열 칸에서는 일관되게 앞섭니다. 다만 평가자가 모델을 만든 쪽이고, DSpine은 블록 16과 동적 검증을 쓰고 DFlash2는 블록 8이라 조건이 완전히 같지 않습니다. 독립 재현이 나오기 전에는 "저자 보고 기준으로 앞선다"까지가 적당합니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;라이선스는 어떤가요?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
카드 헤더에 Apache-2.0이라고 적혀 있고 저장소에 &lt;code&gt;LICENSE&lt;/code&gt; 파일이 있습니다. 상용 사용 전에는 파일 내용과 대상 모델 Qwen3.8-27B의 라이선스를 각각 확인하십시오.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;저희가 직접 돌려 보았나요?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
아닙니다. 이 글은 공개 카드, &lt;code&gt;config.json&lt;/code&gt;, safetensors 헤더, 대상 모델 설정과 HF 메타데이터를 읽고 계산한 결과입니다. 추론을 돌리지 않았고 처리량과 품질은 측정하지 않았습니다. 출처는 &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36173" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv 2609.36173&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://github.com/Tencent/DSpine" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DSpine 저장소&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/tencent/Qwen3.8-27B-DSpine-b16" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;모델 카드&lt;/a&gt;입니다.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://dev.to/vidraft_lab/2biteuraneun-ireumyi-ggufga-3biteu-ireumyi-pailboda-keossseubnida-gonggae-darwin-180b-yangjahwa-pail-6gaereul-hedeoggaji-yeoleo-boni-3k05"&gt;GGUF 이름의 비트 수와 실제 바이트&lt;/a&gt;: 파일 헤더만 읽어 구성 비중을 계산한 같은 방법의 글입니다.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/vidraft_lab/ceung-saie-kv-kaesiga-gyeobcineundedo-julil-ge-eobseossseubnida-loopvq-sosigeul-deudgo-injeob-ceung-jungbogeul-jae-boni-24fd"&gt;층 사이에 KV 캐시가 겹치는데도 줄일 게 없었습니다: LoopVQ 소식을 듣고 인접 층 중복을 재 보니&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Behavioral thermodynamics: a LoRA behavior melts at 3.5× its RMS noise
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a physics-flavoured robustness probe, not a container. Take a fine-tuned behavior,&lt;br&gt;
add Gaussian noise to every LoRA tensor, and measure the order parameter: SST-2 accuracy on&lt;br&gt;
400 rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reproduce:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python research/thermodynamics.py   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# -&amp;gt; research/results/thermodynamics.json&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Host: 4-bit Qwen3B.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Behavior: SST-2 sentiment adapter.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Noise: &lt;code&gt;w + N(0, (σ · RMS)^2)&lt;/code&gt; on every LoRA A/B tensor, one seed per σ.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adapter RMS: &lt;strong&gt;0.016192&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Metric: label-token logit accuracy on 400 SST-2 test rows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The curve
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;σ (× RMS)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;SST-2&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;base (no adapter)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;60.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.0 (clean adapter)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;94.5%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0.5&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;95.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;95.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;94.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;85.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;51.7%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;53.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;half-gain noise&lt;/strong&gt; (accuracy halfway between base 60.8% and adapter 94.5%) is&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;σ = 3.47 × RMS&lt;/strong&gt;. Above that the behavior does not decay gracefully back to the base; it&lt;br&gt;
falls to chance. The adapter's signal is replaced by noise, not by the original model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this looks like
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A plateau, then a cliff.&lt;/strong&gt; From 0 to 2× RMS the behavior is essentially unchanged. At
3× it is damaged; at 5× it is gone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The collapse overshoots the base.&lt;/strong&gt; At high noise SST-2 is ~50%, below the base 60.8%.
The perturbation does not restore the base; it destroys the adapter's contribution and
leaves a chance-level classifier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quantization is far inside the plateau.&lt;/strong&gt; The 8-bit and 4-bit adapters are exact on this
task; their quantization noise is much smaller than the 3.5× RMS meltdown scale.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;This is a first-order-looking curve&lt;/strong&gt;, but one adapter and one task are not enough to
claim a universality class.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Caveats
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gaussian weight noise is not quantization, pruning, or fine-tuning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One adapter, one task, 400 rows, one seed per σ, logit scoring.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The adapter RMS is global; per-tensor noise scales could change the curve.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No finite-size scaling or susceptibility measurement yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Next experiments
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repeat on AG News, Dolly QA and the watermark trigger; does the meltdown scale track
adapter RMS or task margins?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Noise only A, only B, or rank-wise; which part of the LoRA carries the behavior?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Measure the &lt;strong&gt;susceptibility&lt;/strong&gt; (variance of the order parameter across noise seeds) near
the transition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare with quantization: q4/q8 are at a much lower effective temperature; how much
headroom is there before a 3-bit adapter melts?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test whether a &lt;strong&gt;behavioral vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; raises the melting temperature.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The point is not that a LoRA is thermodynamic. The point is that "how much noise can this&lt;br&gt;
behavior survive?" is a measurable curve, and the answer is not "linearly less accuracy per&lt;br&gt;
bit."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Coding assistants are good at well-known networks and tend to guess about smaller ones. To give them something accurate to read about Zucoin's Splitchain layer 1 network, we put the &lt;a href="https://myzucoins.org" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MyZucoins&lt;/a&gt; guides and developer docs behind an open Model Context Protocol (MCP) server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MyZucoins provides tools, services and developer resources for Splitchain. This post shows how to connect the MCP server to Claude or Cursor, and how to call it directly. It needs no account, subscription or API key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: this is written by the team behind MyZucoins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the server does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The endpoint is &lt;code&gt;https://myzucoins.org/mcp&lt;/code&gt; (Streamable HTTP, no authentication). It is read-only content: it cannot access wallets, sign or send transfers, read accounts or give live prices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;search_articles&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search published help, developer tutorials, news and deep dives&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_article&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read a full article by its slug, as plain text&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_topics&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;List topics with article counts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_pages&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Find public Zucoin, Splitchain, wallet, safety and network statistics pages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Connect it to an assistant
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Claude Code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;claude mcp add --transport http myzucoins https://myzucoins.org/mcp
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In Cursor, add it to &lt;code&gt;mcp.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"mcpServers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"myzucoins"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://myzucoins.org/mcp"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then ask something like: "Using MyZucoins, how do I reinstall the Zucoin wallet app without losing my wallet?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Call it directly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MCP over HTTP is JSON-RPC, so you can try it with &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -s -X POST https://myzucoins.org/mcp \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type: application/json'&lt;/span&gt; \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream'&lt;/span&gt; \
-d &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call",
"params":{"name":"search_articles",
"arguments":{"query":"wallet backup","limit":2}}}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Trimmed, one result looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"slug"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"how-do-i-reinstall-the-zucoin-wallet-app"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"kind"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"help"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"How do I reinstall the Zucoin wallet app?"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"excerpt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Back up your current wallet, reinstall the Zucoin wallet app from a preferred source, and restore the wallet from your valid backup file."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"topics"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"troubleshoot"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Troubleshoot"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://myzucoins.org/how-do-i-reinstall-the-zucoin-wallet-app/"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Pass the &lt;code&gt;slug&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;get_article&lt;/code&gt; to read the full guide, with its source link and date.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A few tips for agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call &lt;code&gt;list_topics&lt;/code&gt; to narrow a search, for example to Developer Docs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;next_offset&lt;/code&gt; to page through results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cite the article &lt;code&gt;url&lt;/code&gt; in answers, so people can read the original.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Never ask a user for a wallet backup or private key. The guides themselves warn against sharing them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full documentation is at &lt;a href="https://myzucoins.org/mcp-api-docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;myzucoins.org/mcp-api-docs&lt;/a&gt;. If you build something with it, we'd like to hear what's missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A hardened AOS-CX baseline puts SSH on the management VRF behind an allow-list, authenticates logins centrally with a local fallback, moves SNMP to v3 only, and authenticates NTP. On the access ports it adds port security, BPDU guard or loop protection, DHCP snooping, and ARP inspection, with unused ports shut down in a blackhole VLAN. As on any platform, static-IP cameras need explicit handling before ARP inspection goes on. Plan for that first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The syntax below was checked against the HPE AOS-CX Command-line interface Reference Guide for 8100 and 8360 Switch Series, published February 2026. Other series such as the 6200 and 6300 share most of it, but confirm against the guide for your model and release. Addresses are from the 192.0.2.0/24 documentation range, and every secret is a placeholder. The Cisco equivalent is in &lt;a href="https://hans.study/hardening-cisco-catalyst-switches-ios-xe-baseline/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hardening Cisco Catalyst switches&lt;/a&gt;, and the audit side is in the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/standards-guidance/switch-configuration-audit-checklist-for-security-networks/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch configuration audit checklist&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Management plane
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SSH is disabled until the admin password is set, and it is enabled per VRF. It will not start without it. Put it on the management VRF, restrict who can connect, and turn off Telnet and the web interface where you do not use them. The CLI reference states that the Telnet server is disabled by default, so the explicit line below is a guard against someone enabling it later.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ssh server vrf mgmt
ssh server allow-list
ip 192.0.2.0/24
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
ssh maximum-auth-attempts 3
no telnet server vrf mgmt
no https-server vrf mgmt
session-timeout 10
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Enabling the allow-list restarts the SSH servers and ends active sessions, and an enabled list with no entries blocks everyone. Apply it from the console or from a session inside the permitted range. Test it. Keep the HTTPS server only if you use the REST API or web interface, and then keep it on the management VRF.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication comes next. Create named users in the built-in groups, point SSH and console logins at TACACS+ with local last, and test the fallback before you leave the room. AOS-CX stores local passwords hashed, so there is no separate secret type to choose.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;user ops1 group administrators password
tacacs-server host 192.0.2.20 key plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; vrf mgmt
aaa authentication login ssh group tacacs &lt;span class="kd"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;
aaa authentication login console group tacacs &lt;span class="kd"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;
aaa authentication limit-login-attempts 5 lockout-time 300
banner motd ^
This system is private property of &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;ORGANIZATION&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;. Authorized use only.
Activity is logged and monitored.
^
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide warns that command authorization has no fail-through, so a wrong TACACS+ authorization setup can leave a switch unable to run commands. Always end the group list with local, and prove the setup on a lab switch first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Time, SNMP, and logging
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NTP can be tied to a trusted key so the switch only syncs with a source that proves itself, which protects log timestamps from a spoofed time server on the camera VLAN. SNMPv3 users get authentication and privacy, and the switch can be told to drop v1 and v2c entirely. Syslog goes out over the management VRF.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ntp authentication-key 10 sha1 &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; trusted
ntp authentication
ntp server 192.0.2.30 key 10
ntp vrf mgmt
ntp &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
snmp-server snmpv3-only
snmpv3 security-level auth-privacy
snmpv3 user monitor auth sha256 auth-pass plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; priv aes priv-pass plaintext &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;strong-secret&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; access-level ro
logging 192.0.2.10 severity info vrf mgmt
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide notes that a # character in an NTP key stops synchronization once authentication is on, so choose keys without one. The logging command also supports TCP and TLS transports, and TLS is the better choice when your collector can present a certificate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Control plane basics
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AOS-CX applies a predefined control plane policing policy named default at first startup. Review it with &lt;code&gt;show copp-policy&lt;/code&gt; rather than rewriting it. Leave it alone. Rate limits on ingress ICMP and broadcast traffic on edge ports add a second layer, and the management plane is already limited by the VRF and allow-list above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Access ports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Edge ports get an access VLAN, edge port type, BPDU guard, loop protection, a broadcast rate limit, and port security, all applied the same way to every port that faces a camera, reader, or workstation. The client limit defaults to 1. Sticky learning keeps the first MAC the port sees. A violation shuts the port down, and auto-recovery brings it back after a timer.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;spanning-tree bpdu-guard timeout 300
port-access port-security &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
interface 1/1/1
no routing
vlan access 100
spanning-tree port-type admin-edge
spanning-tree bpdu-guard
loop-protect
rate-limit broadcast 1 percent
port-access port-security &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
port-access security violation action shutdown
port-access security violation action shutdown auto-recovery &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
port-access security violation action shutdown recovery-timer 300
port-access port-security
client-limit 1
sticky-learn &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Port security with one client catches an unmanaged switch added for extra drops, while BPDU guard catches a managed switch or a loop through one. Loop protection is a separate mechanism that sends its own control packets, so it also catches loops that never involve spanning tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DHCP snooping is enabled globally and per VLAN, and only the ports that face the real DHCP server, usually the uplinks or the LAG toward the distribution layer, are trusted. Everything else stays untrusted. From release 10.14 the command is &lt;code&gt;dhcp-snooping&lt;/code&gt;, and earlier releases use &lt;code&gt;dhcpv4-snooping&lt;/code&gt;. ARP inspection is enabled per VLAN on the 8100 and 8360 from 10.12.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dhcp-snooping
vlan 100
dhcp-snooping
arp inspection
interface lag 1
dhcp-snooping trust
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;ARP inspection discards ARP packets that carry an invalid IP-to-MAC binding, so a camera with a static address and no lease is at risk of being dropped. That is the trap. Convert cameras and door controllers to DHCP reservations where you can. For devices that must stay static, the guide documents &lt;code&gt;ipv4 source-binding&lt;/code&gt;, which adds a static entry to the binding database.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ipv4 source-binding 100 192.0.2.50 00:11:22:33:44:55 1/1/5
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The guide documents how to enable ARP inspection and view its trust state per interface, but I could not find a command to set a port as trusted in it. Check the security guide for your release, and keep uplinks in mind when you test, because a wrongly untrusted uplink drops ARP between switches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Unused ports
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unused ports go into a VLAN with no routed interface, get a description, and are shut down.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;vlan 666
name BLACKHOLE
interface 1/1/24
no routing
vlan access 666
description UNUSED
shutdown
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Backups and firmware
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Copy the running configuration to a remote server over SFTP or SCP on a schedule, using the management VRF, and diff each copy against the last. The guide lists the copy forms for running configuration and checkpoints.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cisco_ios"&gt;&lt;code&gt;copy running-config sftp://backup@192.0.2.40/sw01.cfg vrf mgmt
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For firmware, read the release notes and the HPE security bulletins for the target version before upgrading. AOS-CX keeps primary and secondary images, so stage the new image on the secondary partition and keep the known-good one until the switch has run clean for a day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checking your work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/tools/switch-config-audit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch config audit tool&lt;/a&gt; checks a configuration you paste or upload against a published checklist. The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/switch-config-audit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switch config audit&lt;/a&gt; is the remote review across a whole estate, and the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/learning/network-hardening-level-1/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Network Hardening Level 1&lt;/a&gt; course and the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/training/network-and-windows-hardening/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Network and Windows Hardening&lt;/a&gt; training cover the reasoning behind each control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Book a working session at &lt;a href="https://hans.study/book/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;/book/&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


CVE-2026-88984: RBAC Authorization Bypass in Kubeflow Pipelines Multi-User API Server
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability ID:&lt;/strong&gt; CVE-2026-88984&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS Score:&lt;/strong&gt; 7.2&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Published:&lt;/strong&gt; 2026-11-09&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2026-88984 (GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687) is an incorrect authorization vulnerability affecting Kubeflow Pipelines (KFP) prior to version 2.16.1 in multi-user deployments. The multi-user API server treats the empty Kubernetes namespace associated with shared or global pipelines as an unconditional authorization exception across all API verbs. Consequently, any authenticated user can modify, overwrite, or delete shared pipeline definitions and pipeline versions without possessing the required Kubernetes RBAC permissions, compromising the integrity and availability of global machine learning workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An authorization bypass in the Kubeflow Pipelines (&amp;lt; 2.16.1) multi-user API server allows any authenticated user to modify or delete shared (global) pipelines and pipeline versions because the server unconditionally skips Kubernetes RBAC checks when a pipeline's namespace is empty.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Technical Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVE ID&lt;/strong&gt;: CVE-2026-88984 (GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability Class&lt;/strong&gt;: CWE-863: Incorrect Authorization / CWE-285: Improper Authorization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS v4.0 Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 7.2 High (CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:N/VC:N/VI:H/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EPSS Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 0.31% (24.09th Percentile)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Attack Vector&lt;/strong&gt;: Network (Authenticated Low-Privileged Tenant via KFP Multi-User API)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Security Impact&lt;/strong&gt;: Unauthorized Modification and Deletion of Shared/Global Pipelines&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Affected Versions&lt;/strong&gt;: Kubeflow Pipelines &amp;lt; 2.16.1&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CISA KEV Status&lt;/strong&gt;: Not Listed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Affected Systems
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines (multi-user deployments)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-api-server&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;gt;= 2.0.2, &amp;lt; 2.16.1 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;2.16.1&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code Analysis
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/commit/186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;186dd1f&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;fix(backend): enforce pipeline authorization (#13288)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;--- a/backend/src/apiserver/server/pipeline_server.go&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+++ b/backend/src/apiserver/server/pipeline_server.go&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gu"&gt;@@ -1262,8 +1262,21 @@ func (s *BasePipelineServer) canAccessPipeline(ctx context.Context, pipelineId s&lt;/span&gt;
resourceAttributes.Name = pipeline.Name
}
}
&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-   // Skip authentication if the namespace is empty to enable shared pipelines in multi-user mode&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Skip authorization for read-only operations on shared pipelines in multi-user mode.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Write operations (create, update, delete) on shared pipelines must still be authorized&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // against the KFP system namespace, since shared pipelines have no namespace of their own.&lt;/span&gt;
if s.resourceManager.IsEmptyNamespace(resourceAttributes.Namespace) {
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       if resourceAttributes.Verb == common.RbacResourceVerbGet || resourceAttributes.Verb == common.RbacResourceVerbList {&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+           return nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Namespace = common.GetPodNamespace()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Group = common.RbacPipelinesGroup&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Version = common.RbacPipelinesVersion&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       resourceAttributes.Resource = common.RbacResourceTypePipelines&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       err := s.resourceManager.IsAuthorized(ctx, resourceAttributes)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       if err != nil {&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+           return util.Wrapf(err, "Failed to access shared pipeline %s. Check if you have permission to %s shared pipelines", pipelineId, resourceAttributes.Verb)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+       }&lt;/span&gt;
return nil
}
resourceAttributes.Group = common.RbacPipelinesGroup
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Mitigation Strategies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upgrade Kubeflow Pipelines to version 2.16.1 or later to enforce Kubernetes SubjectAccessReview checks against the KFP system namespace for mutating operations on shared pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Configure explicit Kubernetes RoleBindings in the KFP installation namespace (e.g., 'kubeflow') for legitimate administrators or CI/CD service accounts that require write access to global pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deploy Istio AuthorizationPolicy or API gateway rules to restrict mutating HTTP methods (POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH) on pipeline management and multipart upload endpoints (/apis/v1beta1/pipelines/upload*) to trusted administrative identities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audit the KFP metadata database and Envoy/API server access logs for unauthorized pipeline version injections or deletions targeting shared pipelines with empty namespaces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remediation Steps:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the current Kubeflow Pipelines version deployed in the cluster by checking the image tag on the 'ml-pipeline' deployment in the 'kubeflow' namespace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update the KFP manifests or Kustomize overlays to use release 2.16.1 (specifically updating 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-api-server:2.16.1', 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-driver:2.16.1', and 'ghcr.io/kubeflow/kfp-launcher:2.16.1').&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apply a Kubernetes RoleBinding in the KFP system namespace binding the 'kubeflow-edit' ClusterRole to any specific User or ServiceAccount subjects responsible for publishing or maintaining shared pipelines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify the fix by attempting to delete or create a version on a shared pipeline using a standard tenant user token and confirming that the API server returns an authorization error referencing the KFP system namespace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/security/advisories/GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Security Advisory GHSA-ghgh-57qv-h687&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/pull/13288" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines Pull Request #13288: fix(backend): enforce pipeline authorization&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/commit/186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fix Commit 186dd1f3b0403af841c9b4ea7ab94f405339ef6c&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/kubeflow/pipelines/releases/tag/2.16.1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kubeflow Pipelines Release 2.16.1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official CVE.org Record for CVE-2026-88984&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NVD Vulnerability Detail for CVE-2026-88984&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://cvereports.com/reports/CVE-2026-88984" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read the full report for CVE-2026-88984 on our website&lt;/a&gt; for more details including interactive diagrams and full exploit analysis.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: treat &lt;code&gt;Content-Type&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition&lt;/code&gt; as part of the private export contract, persist them with the object, and create the presigned download only after verifying that metadata. A browser opening a PDF or CSV in a tab is usually a response-header problem, not a reason to make the object public.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters in a media system. A tenant may export a CSV of media activity or a PDF review packet, while the storage key should reveal neither the tenant's preferred filename nor its internal account number. The key is an isolation boundary; the download filename is presentation. Mixing those jobs creates confusing bugs and occasionally leaks information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should a private object storage export guarantee?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would record four invariants in the architecture decision record:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Invariant&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Implementation responsibility&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failure it prevents&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The object is private&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Authorization service issues a short-lived presigned link&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A copied URL becomes an uncontrolled public file address&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The bytes have a declared type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exporter selects &lt;code&gt;application/pdf&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;text/csv&lt;/code&gt; from the generated format&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A client guesses from an internal key or a generic binary type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The response asks for a download&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stored metadata includes &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition: attachment&lt;/code&gt; and a safe filename&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The browser opens a PDF or CSV inline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tenant access is checked before signing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The signing service resolves tenant and object ownership from a server-side record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A valid signature is mistaken for a valid business authorization&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last invariant is easy to omit because a presigned URL feels like the authorization step. It is only the delivery credential. It should be minted after the application has established that the requesting user can access that export, and it should carry an expiry appropriate to the handoff. A link sent to a media client is not a replacement for an export record with an owner, format, and status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser is the final observer of the system, so the test needs to reach it. An HTTP client that receives &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and the expected bytes has proved transport. It has not proved the saved filename or whether a PDF opens inline. Small detail, large distinction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do presigned download headers fix the wrong filename and content type?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the generated file, not with the URL. If an export worker produces a PDF, set the media type from that fact. If it produces a CSV, set the CSV type. Do not infer a content type from an untrusted display name, and do not let a storage key such as &lt;code&gt;tenant-1842/job-771&lt;/code&gt; become the customer-facing name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then construct a content disposition that explicitly requests an attachment. A practical filename has two parts: an ASCII fallback for older or limited clients, and an encoded UTF-8 value for names that contain spaces or non-ASCII characters. The fallback should be a basename, never a user-supplied path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the policy layer I would put in front of any storage adapter. It does not know which object-storage implementation signs URLs; that separation is deliberate. The policy can be unit-tested without pretending that a signed link is a browser test.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__future__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;annotations&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unicodedata&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pathlib&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urllib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quote&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;".csv"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text/csv"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;".pdf"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/pdf"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ascii_fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unicodedata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"NFKD"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ascii"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ignore"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;decode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ascii"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sub&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"[^A-Za-z0-9._ -]"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"_"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;" ."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;plain&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"download"&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;export_headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;or&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"filename must be a non-empty basename"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PurePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"only PDF and CSV exports are supported"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ascii_fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;replace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'"'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"_"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;encoded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;quote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;safe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MEDIA_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;suffix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Disposition"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'attachment; filename="{fallback}"; '&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"filename*=UTF-8''{encoded}"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This fixes the policy inputs, not the persisted object by magic. The adapter must apply the returned values while uploading or through its supported metadata update operation, then read the object's metadata before asking for a fresh presigned link. A link issued before the correction belongs to the old delivery attempt; discard it and create another one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a second subtlety: the extension and declared type must agree with the bytes. Renaming a CSV to &lt;code&gt;.pdf&lt;/code&gt; makes the filename look right while leaving the client with the wrong expectation. A useful test fixture contains a real PDF, a real CSV, a name with spaces, a Unicode name, and a long name. I'm not sure which filename limit applies across every browser, proxy, and receiving filesystem, so I would test the actual delivery path instead of publishing a made-up universal limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where does tenant isolation fail in the download path?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most dangerous design is one that checks the tenant once, then passes an object key around as if the key itself were proof. Keys are identifiers, not authorization statements. A download request should resolve an export record such as &lt;code&gt;(tenant_id, export_id, object_key, format, display_name)&lt;/code&gt; and compare the authenticated tenant before signing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A clean critical path looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The export job writes bytes under an opaque key owned by one tenant.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The application stores the format and intended display name beside the export record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The download request checks the authenticated tenant against that record.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The service derives headers from the recorded format and sanitized basename.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The storage adapter verifies the object's metadata and creates a short-lived presigned link.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The browser test checks disposition, filename, media type, and bytes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not let a client submit a replacement &lt;code&gt;Content-Disposition&lt;/code&gt; and treat it as trusted. A client may request a label, but the server owns the final basename and must reject path fragments. Keep the object key opaque as well; an internal key can still disclose sequence numbers or account relationships through logs, referrers, or support screenshots.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The failure modes deserve separate alerts. A PDF displayed inline points at disposition. A downloaded file named after the object key points at filename handling. A CSV downloaded with a PDF association points at the declared type or the generated bytes. A cross-tenant result points at the authorization lookup, regardless of whether the signature itself was valid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One incident can contain more than one failure. That is why I would log a redacted export identifier, tenant identifier, selected format, metadata values, signing timestamp, expiry, and a result category. I would not log the full presigned URL: query parameters are credentials, even when they are short-lived.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which design trade-offs belong in the decision record?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are three reasonable places to enforce download behavior, and they do not provide the same guarantees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Choice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strength&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cost or boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Persist metadata on the object&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every delivery starts from a durable, inspectable contract&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A metadata correction may require a supported rewrite or update, and a new link must be issued&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Add response overrides when signing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Useful when one object needs different presentation names&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The signing adapter must support the override, and stored metadata can still be wrong for other readers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Proxy the download through the application&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Centralizes tenant checks and response headers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application owns bandwidth, range requests, timeouts, and retry behavior&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For private media exports, I prefer durable metadata plus an application authorization check. A response override can be a valid exception for a one-off label, but it should not hide a bad upload contract. A proxy is a valid choice when policy inspection, audit requirements, or content transformation outweigh direct delivery efficiency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is that this pattern is not suitable for every file-delivery product. If the requirement is a permanent public asset, static hosting, or anonymous sharing, a signed-only export path is the wrong abstraction; use a system whose access model explicitly supports that requirement. If the application needs object versioning, immutable retention, conditional writes, or sub-day automatic deletion, those controls must be verified separately rather than assumed from the presence of presigned links. The AWS lifecycle documentation is useful for checking expiry assumptions, and a worker platform may be appropriate for an application-controlled delivery layer, but neither source changes the tenant authorization invariant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should the fix be tested before deployment?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unit tests should cover header derivation and rejection of path-like names. Integration tests should prove that metadata survives the storage adapter and that a new signature is generated after a correction. The browser test is the one teams skip, usually because the API test is green.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would make the browser matrix small and intentional: one PDF and one CSV, each with a plain name, spaces, and Unicode. Assert the suggested filename, whether the browser downloads rather than opens inline, the response media type, and a hash or parser-level check of the bytes. Repeat after link expiry so the client exercises its authorized refresh flow instead of silently falling back to a public URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deployment should also preserve the ordering. Rolling out a new filename policy before the worker and adapter agree on format metadata creates mixed exports, while signing before the authorization record is committed creates a race between visibility and access. A feature flag can limit the new path to a test tenant, but the invariant itself should remain the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision is therefore straightforward: keep tenant authorization in the application, keep bytes and metadata aligned at write time, make attachment disposition explicit, and verify the actual browser behavior. Fixing a filename in frontend code is cosmetic if the response still says inline, and making an object public is an isolation regression disguised as a download fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hi, we're the aiFetchly team. This post is from the team behind aiFetchly — an open-source, local-first desktop AI agent for business automation: &lt;a href="https://www.aifetchly.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.aifetchly.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every small business has tasks that repeat on a clock: the Monday inbox sweep, the Friday invoice check, the weekly summary nobody enjoys writing. They are not hard — they are just always there. That is exactly what scheduled workflows in aiFetchly are for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a scheduled workflow does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You set a task once, with a time and a trigger, and aiFetchly runs it on schedule on your own computer. For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Morning triage (every weekday, 9:00):&lt;/strong&gt; scan new customer mail, sort it by urgency, and draft replies from your local business knowledge base — so you start the day with a prioritised list, not a pile of unread mail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Weekly document round-up (every Friday):&lt;/strong&gt; collect the week's invoices and contracts, sort them into folders, and pull out the key details — amounts, dates, parties — into a short summary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Recurring reports:&lt;/strong&gt; prepare a draft weekly summary from your documents and notes, ready for a human to check and send.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing is sent automatically. A human reviews every draft before anything goes out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why local-first matters here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scheduled work touches your real business data — customer mail, invoices, internal notes. aiFetchly runs locally, and your business knowledge base stays on your own computer. The agent works with your own documents and your own email, Sheets and CRM connections (via plugins), rather than uploading your business to a cloud service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Download aiFetchly from &lt;a href="https://www.aifetchly.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.aifetchly.com&lt;/a&gt; and add your business documents to the local knowledge base.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a scheduled workflow: pick the task, the schedule, and the sources it may use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Let it run once, review the drafts, adjust the instructions, and only then let it become a routine.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;See how the local knowledge base works in practice: &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTGsQ27HsfA" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FTGsQ27HsfA&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pricing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Community edition is free to use. Plus starts at $10/month. (The AI Content Assistant is a Plus-only feature.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What repetitive task would you schedule first — inbox triage, document sorting, or a weekly summary? Let us know in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Press a button in a well-made video game and something happens right away. The character moves, a sound plays, the button glows or the controller vibrates. Players never wonder whether the game registered their input. Game designers treat that instant response as a basic requirement, and players notice at once when it is missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Law firm websites rarely get the same attention. A visitor taps "Call now" and nothing seems to happen for a second. They submit a contact form and the page sits there with no sign it worked. On a criminal defense site, the person tapping is often anxious and in a hurry, so a moment of uncertainty can send them back to the search results. This post covers how to bring game-style feedback to the interactive parts of a law firm site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Respond to every tap within a fraction of a second
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Games aim to respond to input within a frame or two. Jakob Nielsen's long-standing guidance on response times puts the limit for feeling instant at about 0.1 seconds. Anything slower and users start to notice the delay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google measures something similar with Interaction to Next Paint, one of the Core Web Vitals. It tracks how long the page takes to visibly respond after a user interacts, and Google describes 200 milliseconds or less as good. Heavy chat widgets, tracking scripts and large JavaScript bundles are the usual reasons a law firm site responds slowly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start by giving every button a visible pressed state in CSS. That costs almost nothing and tells the user the tap registered even if the next step takes a moment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Show progress during anything that takes time
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Games show loading bars, spinning icons or animations whenever the player has to wait. The player knows the game is working and roughly how long it will take.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a law firm site, form submission is the main wait. When someone submits a contact form, disable the button, change its label to something like "Sending" and show a small spinner. That prevents double submissions and reassures the user. If the request fails, re-enable the button and explain what happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make errors clear and fixable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Good games tell players exactly what went wrong and how to fix it. A locked door has a symbol showing which key it needs. A failed jump shows where the player fell short.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Form errors on many law firm sites do the opposite. They show a generic message at the top of the form or clear every field after a failed submit. Better practice looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Validate fields as the user leaves them, not on every keystroke&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Show the error message next to the field it belongs to&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explain the fix in plain words, such as "Please enter a phone number with area code"&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep everything the user typed when an error appears&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Screen reader users need to hear these messages too. Connect each error to its field and announce status changes in a live region:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;label&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"phone"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Phone&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/label&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"phone"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"phone"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"tel"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-describedby&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"phone-error"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-invalid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"phone-error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Please enter a phone number with area code.&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"form-status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Sending your message...&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Confirm success in a way the user cannot miss
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a player finishes a level, the game makes it obvious with a sound, a screen and often a summary of what happens next. The player is never left wondering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a form submits successfully, replace the form with a clear confirmation message rather than a small line of green text. Tell the user what happens next, such as when they can expect a call and from what number. On a defense site, add a reminder not to share case details by email if the firm prefers to discuss them by phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The form itself matters as much as the feedback around it, and this guide on &lt;a href="https://telegra.ph/Designing-an-Intake-Form-a-Frightened-Caller-Will-Finish-11-03" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;designing an intake form a frightened caller will finish&lt;/a&gt; covers which fields to ask for and which to leave out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test like a game studio
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Game studios watch real players and note every moment of confusion. Law firm sites can do a lighter version. Ask someone who has never seen the site to find the phone number and send a message on their own phone while you watch. Note any pause, repeated tap or confused look.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test on a slow connection as well. Browser developer tools can throttle the network so you see how the site behaves on weak mobile data, which is how many people will reach it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For more on interface lessons drawn from games, the &lt;a href="https://officialsbdmarketing.game.blog/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;UX lessons from video game design&lt;/a&gt; site collects related articles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Questions about feedback on law firm sites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does a pressed state on buttons really matter?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. It is the cheapest way to tell users their tap registered, and it reduces repeated taps that can cause double calls or duplicate form submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I measure Interaction to Next Paint?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PageSpeed Insights and the Chrome User Experience Report show field data for INP when a site has enough traffic. Chrome DevTools can measure it during local testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should a law firm site use sound or vibration like games do?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Visual feedback is enough on a website, and unexpected sound or vibration would feel intrusive to most visitors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SBD Marketing works only with criminal defense law firms, one firm per city. It builds defense firm websites, handles SEO for the charges a firm defends, works on visibility in AI search tools and runs SEO audits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most shopping assistants stop at "try searching for a coupon". We wanted an AI assistant to be able to look up real offers, so we put the &lt;a href="https://secretpass.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SecretPass&lt;/a&gt; offer search behind an open Model Context Protocol (MCP) server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SecretPass is a shopping club with coupon codes and deals for online shopping stores in the United States, the United Kingdom and Australia. This post shows how to connect the MCP server to an assistant, and how to call it directly. It needs no account or API key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: this is written by the team behind SecretPass.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the server does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The endpoint is &lt;code&gt;https://secretpass.com/mcp&lt;/code&gt; (Streamable HTTP, no authentication). It has three read-only tools:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;search_offers&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search offers by keyword, region, category, shop, brand or product type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_offer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read the full public details of one offer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;list_filters&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;List countries, categories, brands and product types with offer counts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Results link to the SecretPass offer page. Voucher codes are not included in MCP results: search is free, and membership unlocks the codes on the website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Connect it to an assistant
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Claude Code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;claude mcp add --transport http secretpass https://secretpass.com/mcp
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In Cursor, add it to &lt;code&gt;mcp.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"mcpServers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"secretpass"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://secretpass.com/mcp"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then ask something like: "Find current shoe offers in the US and compare the savings."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Call it directly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MCP over HTTP is JSON-RPC, so you can try it with &lt;code&gt;curl&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -s -X POST https://secretpass.com/mcp \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type: application/json'&lt;/span&gt; \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream'&lt;/span&gt; \
-d &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call",
"params":{"name":"search_offers",
"arguments":{"query":"shoes","region":"us","limit":2}}}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each offer comes back as structured data. Trimmed, one result looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"us/48237228"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"business"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Marion Parke"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"New pre-fall footwear"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"region"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"us"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"category_name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Retail &amp;amp; Gifts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"offer_display"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"product_type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Shoes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"features"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"15% off the first order."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"expires_at"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://secretpass.com/offer-item/us/48237228"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"membership_required_to_unlock"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Offers change and expire, so have your agent check &lt;code&gt;expires_at&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;updated_at&lt;/code&gt;, and show the user the offer page instead of promising a saving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A few tips for agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call &lt;code&gt;list_filters&lt;/code&gt; first if you need exact brand or product type values.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;limit&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;next_offset&lt;/code&gt; to page through results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;get_offer&lt;/code&gt; with an &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt; to read an offer's conditions before comparing it with others.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full documentation is at &lt;a href="https://secretpass.com/mcp-api-docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;secretpass.com/mcp-api-docs&lt;/a&gt;. If you build something with it, we'd like to hear what's missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’m Omar Zahouri, CEO of NullCry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the next 48 hours, I’m releasing a major NullCry update focused on fixing some of the most important reliability issues in AI-generated projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The update includes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-file truncation fix
Long files will now be accumulated through multiple generation passes without losing previously generated lines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• Support for:&lt;br&gt;
file: path CONTINUED&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;• The longest valid version of each file is preserved.&lt;br&gt;
• A final “Surgical Stitch” reconstructs the complete file from accumulated drafts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fixing “The AI returned no valid files”&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NullCry will now recognize more common AI output formats, including:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;file: index.html&lt;br&gt;
file index.html&lt;br&gt;
index.html&lt;br&gt;
html / javascript / python code blocks&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It will also detect filename/path hints such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;styles.css&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If files still cannot be extracted from a response, NullCry can automatically request the AI to reformat the response into explicit file blocks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;More reliable file continuation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CONT_PROMPT is being improved to continue generation precisely from the interruption point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CONTINUED sections will also be merged automatically instead of being treated as separate incomplete files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Output sanitization improvements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;sanitizeOutput is being improved so legitimate backticks inside source code are not accidentally interpreted as the end of a generated code block.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;NullCry should not just generate code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should reliably understand the requested project, work across files, handle large files, continue interrupted generations, and reconstruct the final project without silently losing code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the next step in making NullCry more reliable for real software development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;— Omar Zahouri&lt;br&gt;
CEO of NullCry&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Email verification looks like a small form until you ship it. Then the edge cases arrive: the request is still running, the code is wrong, the link expired, the user clicked resend twice, or the browser came back from a suspended tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The UI problem is usually an unclear state model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many React forms start with three booleans:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setVerified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is friendly at first, but it can describe impossible combinations. What does &lt;code&gt;loading: true&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;verified: true&lt;/code&gt; mean? Should an old error remain visible while a new request is running? Can the resend button be enabled during a code submission?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part are the transitions, not the number of variables. A verification flow should tell the user what is happening and what action is safe next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Model verification as a small state machine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with named states that match product behavior. A TypeScript discriminated union makes the rules visible in the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"awaiting-code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resendAvailableAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the UI has a smaller decision surface. An &lt;code&gt;awaiting-code&lt;/code&gt; state must carry the timing information needed by the countdown. A &lt;code&gt;verified&lt;/code&gt; state does not need an error message. The compiler helps keep these informations together when the flow changes later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I like this approach because it connects product language to implementation language. “Check your inbox” becomes &lt;code&gt;awaiting-code&lt;/code&gt;, while “Your code expired” becomes &lt;code&gt;expired&lt;/code&gt;. This makes a confusing email flow a little more clear for both the user and the next developer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Render states with a discriminated union
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep rendering close to the state model, but keep side effects in event handlers or a dedicated hook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;your&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"awaiting-code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CodeForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Checking&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;You&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;can&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"alert"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;again&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The UI should not guess from a partially updated collection of flags. It should announce the current state, disable actions that cannot succeed, and preserve focus when an error replaces the form. A status message that appears only visually is not enough; screen readers need a useful announcement too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep resend and expiration explicit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resend is where many otherwise clean implementations become flaky. Treat it as a separate transition with a server-side policy behind it. The client countdown is useful feedback, but it is not the source of truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the user submits a code, capture the request identity. If an older request finishes after a newer one, ignore the stale result. A simple sequence number works for a small hook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;verify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;verifyCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"That code is not valid."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This small guard prevents a slow response from moving a fresh form backwards. It also make the failure behavior much easier to explain in a test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For temporary test inboxes, a service such as &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tempmailso&lt;/a&gt; can help isolate verification data during development. Do not use an inbox you do not control for real customer accounts, and do not treat a successful test message as proof that production delivery is healthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test the transitions, not just the happy path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Component tests should cover state changes and user-visible consequences:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sending starts with one click and announces progress.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A successful response renders the code form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An invalid code keeps the user in a recoverable error state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A stale response cannot overwrite a newer request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resend is unavailable during its cooldown and becomes available afterward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expiration offers a clear next action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also where &lt;a href="https://dev.to/jasonmills94/tie-eks-alerts-to-one-rollout-4kei"&gt;connecting one alert to one rollout&lt;/a&gt; is a useful operational idea: make each failure point explainable instead of collapsing several causes into “Something went wrong.” For account flows, &lt;a href="https://dev.to/sophiax99/safer-email-change-flows-start-with-proof-11ea"&gt;proof-first email change flows&lt;/a&gt; offer the same mindset from a security angle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During manual testing, try a tem email, paste a code twice, navigate away while the request is pending, and return after the expiry time. These cases finds the gaps that a single successful test will miss. They are boring checks, but boring checks is exactly what protects a sign-up flow from surprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A short shipping checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before shipping, check that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every visible message maps to one explicit state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Submit, resend, and continue actions have clear disabled rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stale responses cannot overwrite current state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expiration and retry behavior are tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Status and error messages work with keyboard and screen readers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The server still enforces verification expiry and resend limits.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An explicit state machine does not make email delivery perfect. It does make the product honest about what it knows, what it is waiting for, and what the user can do next. That clarity is a feature worth shipping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A user opens a page, sees a button, and clicks it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your automated test opens the same page, sees the same button, and fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the button is replaced a moment later. Sometimes a loading skeleton looks enough like the real component to confuse a selector. Sometimes the browser's address bar changes the available space, and an element moves just as the test tries to click it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application might be broken. The test might be broken. Or both might be behaving exactly as designed, just at different moments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's what makes modern browser testing frustrating. We build increasingly dynamic interfaces, then write tests as if pages appear all at once and stay put.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A visible page isn't necessarily an interactive page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider an app using React Suspense and server-side rendering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server sends HTML. The browser displays it. A person looking at the screen thinks the page is ready. But JavaScript may still be loading, data may still be resolving, and React may still be attaching event handlers or replacing parts of the DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your test finds the Save button and clicks it. Nothing happens. So someone adds a two-second wait.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That seems to fix it, until CI gets a little slower.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The better approach is to test readiness, not elapsed time. Wait for the specific control to become usable or for the application to expose the state that means the interaction can succeed. If a component passes through loading, hydrated, and interactive states, make those states observable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This &lt;a href="https://vibiumlabs.com/how-to-make-browser-tests-survive-react-suspense-streaming-ssr-and-partial-hydration/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to testing React Suspense, streaming SSR, and partial hydration&lt;/a&gt; lays out the important distinction between slow data, replaced DOM nodes, and lost focus. Those failures can look identical from the outside, but they need different fixes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A longer sleep is not a diagnosis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Your selector may be perfectly valid and still be wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now imagine a dashboard with a list of customer accounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The test clicks the third row. It worked yesterday. Today, a new account arrives and the list reorders automatically. The third row is now a different customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The selector didn't fail. That's worse. It worked on the wrong thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skeleton loaders create another version of this problem. A test sees the container, assumes the content is ready, and attempts to interact with something that hasn't been rendered yet. Virtualized lists make it even trickier because rows may not exist in the DOM until they're scrolled into view.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful &lt;a href="https://bugbench.com/benchmark-plan-comparing-browser-automation-frameworks-on-dynamic-list-reordering-skeleton-loaders-and-selector-resilience/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;benchmark plan for dynamic lists, skeleton loaders, and selector resilience&lt;/a&gt; suggests measuring more than pass rate. It also looks at how often selectors need rewriting, how many special waits are required, and whether failures explain what actually happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That seems like a much better way to compare testing frameworks than clicking the same static login form 1,000 times.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For application tests, I would rather target an account by its stable identifier or accessible name than by its position on the page. And I would rather wait for a meaningful loaded state than assume the disappearance of one spinner proves everything is ready.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checkout isn't one page. It's a series of handoffs.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Payment flows expose the limits of simplistic browser tests particularly well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A checkout form might live inside a third-party iframe. Then the provider redirects the user to a 3D Secure challenge. The browser returns to your site, perhaps after a reload, and the application has to recover the original order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A test that only enters card details and looks for a success message can miss the worst failures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Was the original payment intent preserved? Did the order remain associated with the correct customer? What happens if someone refreshes during the challenge or submits twice? Could you create a duplicate charge or order?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://automated-testing-services.com/how-to-test-payment-iframe-flows-without-losing-state-across-reloads-redirects-and-3ds-challenges/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to testing payment iframes, redirects, and 3DS flows&lt;/a&gt; approaches checkout as a state-preservation problem, not just a sequence of clicks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the right mental model. Use a payment provider's sandbox or test environment, exercise the redirect and challenge paths, and verify the backend result. The browser reaching a thank-you page is useful evidence, but it isn't proof that the payment was processed exactly once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


On mobile, even the viewport is moving
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Desktop tests have a fairly stable idea of how much screen space is available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mobile browsers are different. The address bar expands and collapses. The on-screen keyboard appears. Safe-area insets protect content around notches and system UI. A fixed footer that looked fine in one screenshot can cover the only button a customer needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where pixel-perfect assertions can become counterproductive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://frontendtester.com/how-to-test-viewport-units-safe-area-insets-and-mobile-browser-chrome-changes-without-flaky-layout-assertions/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;practical guide to viewport units and safe-area testing&lt;/a&gt; makes a useful point: test whether content remains reachable and the layout responds correctly, not whether every coordinate remains identical after the browser chrome changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a mobile checkout, I'd want to know whether the Pay button stays visible or scrollable into view, whether the keyboard obscures an input, and whether fixed elements overlap important content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are customer problems. A three-pixel difference in an unrelated margin usually isn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Visual tests need a review process, not just screenshots
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Visual regression testing sounds simple: take a screenshot, compare it with the baseline, and flag differences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then your team updates a shared button component and gets dozens of screenshot changes. Some are intentional. Some are real regressions. Someone has to tell them apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more noisy the system gets, the easier it is to approve a change without looking carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://testautomationreviews.com/best-visual-testing-tools-for-design-system-teams-that-need-low-noise-and-clear-review-workflows/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;comparison of visual testing tools for design system teams&lt;/a&gt; evaluates the parts that matter after a difference is detected: noise control, baseline management, pull-request context, approvals, and ongoing maintenance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the interesting buying decision. Not just whether a tool can find differences, but whether people can review those differences quickly enough to keep trusting it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test the transitions, not just the destinations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I were evaluating a browser testing setup today, I'd make a small collection of deliberately annoying scenarios before writing a large suite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A page that hydrates after the HTML appears. A list that reorders during interaction. A payment flow that leaves the site and returns. A mobile footer that has to survive changing viewport height. A component update that produces a lot of legitimate visual differences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I'd ask a few plain questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Can the tests complete without arbitrary sleeps? When something fails, can we tell whether the app or the test is wrong? How much work does it take to maintain the selectors and review the screenshots?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Passing the happy path matters. But real applications spend a lot of time between states: loading, updating, redirecting, returning, resizing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where customers find bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's also where your tests need to earn their keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine this scenario:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A customer clicks &lt;strong&gt;Place Order&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The request reaches your server, the database successfully creates the order, but the response never reaches the browser because of a network issue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The customer assumes something went wrong and clicks again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now you have &lt;strong&gt;two orders instead of one&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a common reliability problem in web applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disabling a submit button might prevent accidental double-clicks, but it doesn't guarantee that an operation will execute only once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's explore how to handle this properly using React, Node.js, and PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Frontend protection is only the first layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In React, we often prevent repeated submissions using a loading state:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is useful for improving the user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there is a problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Duplicate requests can still happen because of:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Network retries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple browser tabs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Concurrent API calls&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repeated webhook deliveries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Client or server failures&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even rapid calls within the same render cycle can observe the same React state before an update takes effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The important lesson: frontend protection improves UX, but backend protection ensures data integrity.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Understanding idempotency
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An operation is idempotent when performing it multiple times has the same intended effect as performing it once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /api/orders
Idempotency-Key: order-abc-123
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the same request is submitted again with the same idempotency key, the server should recognize that the operation has already been processed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of creating a new order, it can return the result of the original operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is particularly important for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Order creation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Payment processing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Subscription activation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inventory adjustments&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Financial transactions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Implementing idempotency in React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We can generate a unique idempotency key using the browser's built-in Web Crypto API.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OrderForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Generate once for this intended operation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/api/orders"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Idempotency-Key"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Order request failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Use createOrder in your submission flow.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The key detail is that &lt;strong&gt;the same idempotency key must be reused when retrying the same intended operation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate a new key only when the user starts a genuinely new order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a real application, you may need to persist the key beyond the component lifecycle if an unfinished operation must survive a page refresh.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Protecting the database against duplicate inserts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend protection and idempotency middleware are useful, but concurrent requests can still introduce race conditions if the database isn't protected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider this backend logic:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;existingOrder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;findOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;existingOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Looks fine, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two requests can execute simultaneously:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Request A                  Request B
|                          |
Check existing order       Check existing order
|                          |
Not found                  Not found
|                          |
Create order               Create order
|                          |
Success                    Success
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both requests can pass the check before either creates the record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a classic check-then-insert race condition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


A better approach: Database uniqueness constraints
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In PostgreSQL, enforce uniqueness at the database level.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNIQUE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;INDEX&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_order_request&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This assumes your orders table contains the corresponding columns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now use PostgreSQL's &lt;code&gt;ON CONFLICT&lt;/code&gt; functionality:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;INSERT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;INTO&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;product_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;request_hash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;VALUES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;CONFLICT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;DO&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOTHING&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RETURNING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the insert succeeds, the query returns the new order ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If another request already used the same key, PostgreSQL prevents a duplicate insert.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend can then retrieve the existing order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One additional safeguard is important: compare the stored request hash with the incoming request's hash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the same idempotency key is reused with different order parameters, return a conflict response instead of silently accepting the changed request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Database constraints provide protection that application-level checks alone cannot guarantee.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. What about transactions?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preventing duplicate orders is only part of the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Creating an order might involve multiple operations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create the order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reserve inventory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update related database records.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record the transaction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a notification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If one operation fails halfway through, the system can end up in an inconsistent state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Database transactions help ensure that related database modifications succeed or fail together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, external operations such as sending emails or publishing messages are not automatically rolled back with a database transaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For these cases, patterns such as the &lt;strong&gt;transactional outbox&lt;/strong&gt; and idempotent event consumers can improve reliability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an important distinction when designing systems that interact with external services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Test real failure scenarios
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A feature working during normal testing doesn't necessarily mean it is reliable under failure conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are five scenarios worth testing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Scenario&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Expected behavior&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two concurrent requests with the same key&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Only one order is created&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retry after the original request commits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Existing order is returned&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same key with different request data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Request is rejected&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Failed database transaction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No partial order is committed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two legitimate operations with different keys&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both can proceed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These tests provide more confidence than simply checking whether a submit button becomes disabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. The approach I recommend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For reliable applications, multiple layers should work together:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Frontend safeguards&lt;/strong&gt; improve the user experience and discourage accidental repeated submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Backend idempotency&lt;/strong&gt; identifies repeated attempts to perform the same operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Database constraints&lt;/strong&gt; protect against duplicate inserts, including concurrent requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transactions and carefully designed side effects&lt;/strong&gt; help maintain consistency across the entire workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No single layer solves every problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal isn't just to make an API request succeed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's to make sure retries, failures, and concurrent requests don't produce unintended results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Final thoughts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building reliable software requires thinking beyond the happy path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A submit button can be disabled. A network request can fail. A server can process the same operation more than once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application should still behave predictably.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A combination of frontend safeguards, idempotency, database constraints, and transaction handling is generally more reliable than depending on any one of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What's your approach to preventing duplicate requests in production applications?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Further reading:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PostgreSQL INSERT / ON CONFLICT documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've never had so much power to build software. You can turn an idea into a working app in a day/hour/minute? But there's always a catch: You AI agent can solve it too. So the question is: &lt;strong&gt;does anyone actually need what we/you/me're building?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been working in product development, open source, and startups for years, and this question has never felt more closer to home.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every day we see more and more apps, but it's not always clear who the end user is or what problem it's going solving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've been working on our data grid library, &lt;a href="https://rv-grid.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RevoGrid&lt;/a&gt;, for quite a few years now (closer to decade if we count for Open Source part). It's an Excel-like data grid you can put into your app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over this time, we've built so many components and tried so many approaches that you could probably build an entire Power BI on top of it. We've spent even more time testing it and solving quests of "what to do next?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So recently, we spent a week of our lives extracting these components into a &lt;a href="https://ui.rv-grid.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;separate UI library&lt;/a&gt; and making it available to everyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What's inside?&lt;/strong&gt; Pretty much the building blocks we've needed for our own products: charts, AI chat interfaces, file viewers, date pickers, dropdowns, forms, time grids, timeline controls, and plenty of smaller UI components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Basically, the stuff you need when building analytical dashboards, reporting tools, or project management apps. And it works with React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, and plain TypeScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We use it a lot ourselves. Pretty much all our interfaces are built with these components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not sure if the world needs another UI library, but here it is. It's simple, it's been tested in our own products, and maybe it'll find a place in yours too, especially if you don't want to do all the heavy lifting yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Happy coding! (Or is it vibe coding now?)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In January I posted a crypto bubble chart I'd built from scratch on Threads. Few people saw it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ten months, one redesign and a lot of tests later, it's a library: &lt;strong&gt;OrbFlow&lt;/strong&gt;. It's a bubble chart for the web where the bubbles act like bubbles. They rise in when the chart scrolls into view, float, push each other apart, and pop when you tap one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today the beta is on npm, and I'd like you to try to break it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it in two minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;npm i orbflow@beta&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt; (18 or 19, works with Next.js):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { OrbChart } from "orbflow/react";

export default function App() {
return (
&amp;lt;div style={{ width: 600, height: 400 }}&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;OrbChart
data={[
{ id: "a", label: "Apples", value: 40 },
{ id: "b", label: "Pears", value: 25 },
{ id: "c", label: "Plums", value: 10 },
]}
onOrbClick={(orb) =&amp;gt; console.log(orb.label)}
/&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
);
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plain JavaScript:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { OrbChart } from "orbflow";

const chart = new OrbChart("#chart");
chart.setData([
{ id: "a", label: "Apples", value: 40 },
{ id: "b", label: "Pears", value: 25 },
]);
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give the container a size and the chart fills it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's in the box
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Zero runtime dependencies, about 15 kB gzipped&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bubble area follows the value, so twice the value really looks twice as big&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tap to pop, with droplets, and popped bubbles can come back&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keyboard and screen reader support: every bubble is a real button in a hidden list&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Respects "reduce motion": the chart settles and stays still&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TypeScript types, ESM, CommonJS, and a CDN script&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a beta
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once it's called 1.0, the API is a promise, and renaming anything after that breaks your app. So before I make that promise, I want to hear what feels wrong. The things I most want feedback on:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Were the prop and option names what you expected?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Did anything about sizing or layout surprise you?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What data did you try, and did it read well as bubbles?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's left before v1
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Docs and API reference pass&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Runnable examples&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] A speed pass for big charts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Your feedback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open an issue on &lt;a href="https://github.com/cascadingGeek/OrbFlow/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; or comment here. Everyone whose feedback changes something gets credited in the v1 release notes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm building the rest in public on &lt;a href="https://www.threads.com/@cascadinggeek" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Threads&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://x.com/_cascadinggeek" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;X&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


How Does BreathTime’s Petition System Actually Work?
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BreathTime is a small civic platform: people propose petitions, sign them, and leave a few words of support. This post walks through exactly how each of those steps works today — including the parts that are deliberately simple, and the parts that don't exist yet.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Step 1: Proposing a Petition
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Any registered, logged-in user can suggest a petition through the &lt;strong&gt;Propose a Petition&lt;/strong&gt; page. A proposal has three fields:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;title&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;description&lt;/strong&gt; of what should change and why&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;addressee&lt;/strong&gt; — the government, institution or organisation the petition is aimed at&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The form is a React page that sends the proposal to an API route; the server checks that the user is signed in (NextAuth) and saves it to the database (Prisma + MySQL) with the status &lt;em&gt;pending&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Step 2: Moderation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every proposal is read by a person. In the admin panel, proposals are listed newest first, with the author's name. For each one, the administrator can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;approve&lt;/strong&gt; it, or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;reject&lt;/strong&gt; it,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;optionally adding a comment. Either way, the author gets an in-app notification — and on a rejection, the admin's comment is included so they know why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Approval is a decision, not an automatic switch: a live petition is then published by an administrator, who sets its title, description, addressee, category and signature goal (1,000 by default). Users can't publish petitions directly — that's on purpose for a platform this young. Every petition that goes live has been looked at by a human first.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Step 3: Signing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each petition has its own page (&lt;code&gt;/petitions/&amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;), rendered on the server with Next.js's App Router. When someone presses &lt;strong&gt;Sign&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;You must be logged in.&lt;/strong&gt; Guests can't sign; the request is refused until you sign in or register. The petition also has to be active.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One person, one signature.&lt;/strong&gt; The server checks whether you've already signed, and the database enforces it too, with a unique constraint on &lt;em&gt;petition + user&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The signature is saved.&lt;/strong&gt; It's stored together with an update to the petition's signature count, and the petition's author receives a notification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your page refreshes.&lt;/strong&gt; You see the new count straight away. Other visitors see it the next time they load the page — there are no live, real-time updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Step 4: Leaving a Comment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you sign, you can add an optional comment — a sentence on why it matters to you — and choose whether your signature is public. Public signatures, with their comments, are shown under the petition (the most recent 20).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no separate discussion thread yet, and no automatic word filter: comments are attached to signatures, and only people who have signed can leave one.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


What Isn't There (Yet)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An honest list, because it's an early-stage platform:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No pagination&lt;/strong&gt; in the admin proposal list — fine at today's volume, not forever.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No audit log&lt;/strong&gt; of admin decisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No real-time updates&lt;/strong&gt; — counts and comments refresh on page load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No automated moderation&lt;/strong&gt; — every proposal is reviewed by a person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


The Stack Behind It
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Next.js 16 (App Router)&lt;/strong&gt; — server-rendered petition pages and API routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prisma 5 + MySQL 8&lt;/strong&gt; — separate tables for petitions, signatures and proposals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;NextAuth v4&lt;/strong&gt; (credentials + JWT) — sign-in and sessions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS v4&lt;/strong&gt; — the interface, available in 48 languages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Three.js&lt;/strong&gt; — the animated globe on the homepage: yellow dots for the locations of registered users, green for users active in the last 15 minutes, and your own dot green when you're logged in, red when you're not&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


A Real Example
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first petition on the platform is the one BreathTime is named after: &lt;strong&gt;“BreathTime – globalny dzień oddechu dla naszej planety”&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;em&gt;a global day of breath for our planet&lt;/em&gt;), addressed to the Polish government. It proposes that once a month, on the second Sunday, we pause commercial flights, cut combustion-vehicle traffic and scale back non-critical industrial production — as a constructive alternative to disruptive protest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the time of writing it has &lt;strong&gt;8 signatures&lt;/strong&gt; towards a goal of 1,000. Small numbers, real people — which is exactly where every petition starts.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Wrapping Up
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Propose, review, sign, comment: four steps, each kept deliberately simple. If you have a cause worth putting in front of decision-makers, &lt;a href="https://breathtime.info/proposals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;propose a petition&lt;/a&gt; — and if you think the process itself could work better, tell us.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Server-Side Rendering on Country Subdomains: What We Got Wrong First
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;24ad.info is a classifieds site where every country has its own subdomain — &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;de.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gb.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; — all served by one app. The frontend is a React 19 single-page app built with Vite, running on Express behind Caddy, with everything behind Cloudflare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single-page app has a well-known weakness: the first HTML a visitor (or a search engine) receives is mostly empty. Getting real content and correct metadata into that first response, &lt;em&gt;per country&lt;/em&gt;, took a few rounds. Here they are, including the one we rolled back the same day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Round 1 (April): canonical URLs and the geo-redirect
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first problems were about which URL counts as "the" page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canonical URLs always pointed at the main domain.&lt;/strong&gt; A category page on &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; told search engines its canonical address was the home page of &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;. On 21 April that changed: the canonical now uses the host the request actually came in on, so &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info/c/…&lt;/code&gt; points to itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The geo-redirect.&lt;/strong&gt; Visitors who arrive at the bare &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; are sent to their country's subdomain, using Cloudflare's country header; countries we don't support go to the UK site (&lt;code&gt;gb.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;). The redirect became a permanent &lt;code&gt;301&lt;/code&gt; instead of a temporary &lt;code&gt;302&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Who should &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; be redirected.&lt;/strong&gt; Search-engine bots, logged-in users (an admin choosing the global view kept being bounced to their own country), and anyone who explicitly picks &lt;em&gt;Global&lt;/em&gt; — that choice adds &lt;code&gt;?global=1&lt;/code&gt;, which the redirect respects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Round 2 (May): injecting data — and rolling half of it back
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 19 May, version 1.4.3 started &lt;strong&gt;injecting data into the first response&lt;/strong&gt;. For the home page and for individual listings, the server fetched the data up front and embedded it in the HTML; the React app picked it up as its initial state. The result: no loading spinner, and the right &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and meta tags from the very first byte.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For listing pages it worked. For the home page it caused a real bug: home shows the newest listings, and &lt;strong&gt;listings are country-specific&lt;/strong&gt; — but the injected data wasn't. Visitors could get a mix of UK and global listings on their own country's home page. The home-page injection was removed the same day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson was simple: anything you pre-render on a multi-country site has to be scoped by country exactly as the live page would be, or it's worse than nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where it stands now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today the server renders three kinds of page with their own metadata and embedded initial data:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the &lt;strong&gt;home page&lt;/strong&gt;,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;listing pages&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;/posts/:slug&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;category pages&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;/c/:slug&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;with listings &lt;strong&gt;filtered by the country subdomain&lt;/strong&gt; the request came in on. Every other route gets the app's HTML template with its own canonical URL and meta tags. Unknown paths return a real &lt;code&gt;404&lt;/code&gt; marked &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt;, instead of quietly showing the home page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are no placeholders in the template: the server replaces the existing title, description, Open Graph and canonical tags, and adds a block of initial data. The template itself is never cached; server-rendered pages are cached at the Cloudflare edge for 60 seconds (with a further 5 minutes of serving stale while refreshing), and that cache is purged on every deploy. Static assets under &lt;code&gt;/assets&lt;/code&gt; are served directly by Caddy with a one-year immutable cache.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One Caddy site block covers both &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;*.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, using a wildcard certificate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we'd tell anyone doing the same
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canonical URLs are per host&lt;/strong&gt;, not per site, once you use subdomains.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Decide who the geo-redirect must leave alone&lt;/strong&gt; — bots, logged-in users and explicit choices — before you ship it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pre-rendered data must be scoped exactly like the live page.&lt;/strong&gt; If the live page filters by country, the server must too — or don't pre-render it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; continues to evolve as developers build increasingly complex web applications. Learning the fundamentals is important, but professional developers also need to understand modern development tools, architectural approaches, and emerging best practices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Future-proofing ReactJS skills does not mean learning every new library immediately. It means understanding the underlying concepts and knowing how to select appropriate technologies for &lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Feo5vrd1uiv0nzdtgmdmi.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Feo5vrd1uiv0nzdtgmdmi.png" alt=" " width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;different projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Modern JavaScript&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Strong JavaScript knowledge is the foundation of advanced React development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand concepts such as destructuring, spread operators, modules, promises, async and await, closures, and array methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, methods such as map(), filter(), and reduce() are frequently used when working with application data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these concepts helps developers write cleaner React components and solve complex programming problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn TypeScript with React&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TypeScript adds static typing to JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It helps developers identify certain errors during development rather than waiting until the application runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React developers can use TypeScript to define component props, state structures, event types, and API response formats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;type UserProps = {&lt;br&gt;
name: string;&lt;br&gt;
age: number;&lt;br&gt;
};&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function UserCard({ name, age }: UserProps) {&lt;br&gt;
return (&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;{name}&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;p&gt;{age}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
);&lt;br&gt;
}

&lt;p&gt;This makes component expectations clearer and improves development experience in larger projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explore Modern React Features&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should stay familiar with modern React capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Features such as transitions, Suspense, Server Components, and improved rendering APIs support different application requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Server Components allow compatible frameworks and environments to render certain components on the server without shipping their component implementation to the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand the distinction between server and client components and the restrictions associated with each.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn a Modern React Framework&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is a library for building user interfaces, and developers often use additional tools to build complete applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frameworks such as Next.js provide features including routing, server rendering, static generation, and server-side functionality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these capabilities helps developers build applications that go beyond isolated frontend components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the choice of framework should depend on project requirements rather than popularity alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Development with Vite&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vite is a modern frontend build tool that provides fast development startup and efficient development workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It supports React projects and offers features such as hot module replacement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use it to create projects, organize source files, and configure development environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding build tools is important because modern React applications rely on bundling, transformation, and optimization processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow Accessibility Standards&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessibility should be an essential part of every development project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use semantic HTML elements, appropriate labels, keyboard navigation, and sufficient colour contrast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ARIA attributes should be used when necessary to communicate additional information to assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessible applications are easier to use and provide a better experience for a wider audience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Server-Side Rendering&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server-Side Rendering (SSR) allows web pages to be rendered on the server before being delivered to the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can improve initial content availability and help search engines discover page content, depending on the application's implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also understand hydration, which connects the server-rendered HTML to the client-side React application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Incorrect server and client rendering assumptions can lead to hydration errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build Real-World Projects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading documentation is important, but practical experience is equally valuable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should build projects that involve different React concepts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An e-commerce product catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A task management application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A weather dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A personal portfolio.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An employee management system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A blog with search and filtering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These projects help developers understand how components, state, routing, APIs, and testing work together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn Git and Collaborative Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Professional development often involves working with other developers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Git helps teams track code changes, manage branches, review modifications, and collaborate on projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand commits, branches, merges, pull requests, and conflict resolution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These skills are important for working effectively in professional software development environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep Learning Through Documentation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technology changes quickly, and developers should develop the habit of reading official documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The official React documentation is an excellent resource for understanding modern React concepts and recommended practices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Experimenting with small examples and reviewing existing projects can also improve technical understanding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing functional code is only one part of successful application development. Developers must also ensure that their applications behave correctly under different conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing plays an important role in identifying errors, preventing unexpected behaviour, and maintaining application quality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; developers can use several testing techniques to verify individual components, user interactions, and complete application workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand the Importance of Component Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications are built using reusable components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A component may display information, accept user input, or perform specific actions when users interact with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Component testing helps developers verify whether individual components behave as expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a login button should respond correctly when clicked, while an input field should accept and display the entered value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing components independently makes it easier to identify problems before they affect the entire application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Jest for JavaScript Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jest is a widely used JavaScript testing framework.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It provides features for writing test cases, checking expected results, mocking functions, and measuring code coverage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use Jest to test utility functions, data-processing logic, and other JavaScript operations used within React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function add(a, b) {&lt;br&gt;
return a + b;&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;test("adds two numbers", () =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This simple test checks whether the function produces the expected output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explore React Testing Library&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Testing Library helps developers test components from the user's perspective.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than focusing on internal implementation details, it encourages testing what users can actually see and interact with.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, developers can verify whether a heading appears, a button responds to a click, or an error message is displayed after an invalid form submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach creates tests that are more closely connected to actual application behaviour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test User Interactions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing user interactions is important because many application features depend on user input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clicking buttons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Submitting forms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Selecting dropdown options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Opening modal windows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigating between pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Filtering and sorting information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The user-event utility from Testing Library can simulate realistic user interactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should verify both the action and its expected result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mock API Requests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Components that communicate with external APIs should be tested under different response conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mocking allows developers to simulate successful requests, server errors, and delayed responses without depending on an actual backend service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as Mock Service Worker (MSW) can intercept network requests and provide controlled responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes tests more predictable and easier to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement End-to-End Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unit and component tests are valuable, but they do not always reveal problems involving multiple parts of an application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;End-to-end testing checks complete user workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce test may involve opening a product page, adding an item to the cart, entering checkout details, and completing an order through a test environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as Playwright and Cypress are useful for automating these workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test Error and Loading Conditions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should not test only successful scenarios.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reliable application must also handle failed requests, missing information, invalid inputs, and unavailable services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tests should verify whether the application displays appropriate loading indicators and meaningful error messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This helps prevent unexpected behaviour in real-world environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Measure Code Coverage Carefully&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code coverage indicates how much of the application's code is executed during testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can help identify untested sections, but high coverage does not automatically mean that an application is free from bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should focus on meaningful test cases rather than writing unnecessary tests simply to achieve a particular percentage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Include Testing in the Development Workflow&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing should be integrated into the regular development process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated tests can run whenever developers submit code changes through a continuous integration pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This helps identify problems early and reduces the risk of introducing regressions into production applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forms are an essential feature of modern web applications. Users interact with forms to register accounts, submit feedback, place orders, update personal information, and complete online transactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although &lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; provides basic tools for handling forms, complex forms require a more organized approach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Advanced form-handling techniques help developers reduce unnecessary code, improve validation, and create a better user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Controlled Components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A controlled component is an input element whose value is managed through React state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function LoginForm() {&lt;br&gt;
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;return (&lt;br&gt;

type="email"&lt;br&gt;
value={email}&lt;br&gt;
onChange={event =&amp;gt; setEmail(event.target.value)}&lt;br&gt;
/&amp;gt;&lt;br&gt;
);&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React controls the input value, making it easier to validate information and respond to user interactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Controlled components are particularly useful for forms that require conditional fields or real-time feedback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Uncontrolled Components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Uncontrolled components store their current values in the DOM rather than React state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can access these values through refs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach may be useful for simple forms or when integrating with certain third-party libraries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choosing between controlled and uncontrolled components depends on the complexity of the form and the application's requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Formik or React Hook Form&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing validation and submission logic manually for every form can create repetitive code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as React Hook Form and Formik simplify form management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Hook Form is particularly useful for reducing unnecessary re-renders and managing complex form inputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use these libraries to handle registration forms, checkout pages, surveys, and multi-step applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Schema-Based Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Schema-based validation allows developers to define rules separately from the user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as Zod and Yup can define conditions for email addresses, passwords, phone numbers, and other fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a registration form might require a valid email address and a password containing a minimum number of characters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Centralized validation makes it easier to maintain consistent rules across multiple forms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide Real-Time Feedback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users should understand why an input is invalid and how to correct it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an email field can display a clear error message when the entered value does not match the expected format.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, developers should avoid overwhelming users with too many messages while they are still typing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validation timing should depend on the field and the overall form experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create Multi-Step Forms&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some applications require lengthy forms containing multiple sections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of displaying every field on one screen, developers can divide the form into steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an online application may collect personal details, educational information, and supporting documents separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application should preserve entered information when users move between steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should also provide progress indicators and allow users to review their answers before final submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Asynchronous Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Certain validation rules require communication with a server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an application may need to check whether a username is already registered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should handle loading indicators, server errors, and delayed responses carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Debouncing can reduce unnecessary API requests when users type into fields that require remote validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Accessibility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every input should have an associated label.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error messages should be descriptive, and important validation information should be available to screen readers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also ensure that users can navigate forms using a keyboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessible forms are easier to use for everyone, not just users relying on assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Secure Form Submission&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend validation improves usability but cannot replace backend validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All submitted information should be checked again on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sensitive information should be transmitted through HTTPS, and applications should implement suitable protections against malicious submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigation is an essential part of every modern web application. Users expect to move between pages, access specific sections, and return to previously visited content without unnecessary page reloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Router&lt;/a&gt; is a widely used routing library that helps developers manage navigation in React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although basic routing is relatively easy to implement, advanced routing concepts provide greater flexibility when developing large applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Client-Side Routing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traditional websites often request a completely new HTML page whenever a user clicks a navigation link.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications commonly use client-side routing, where the browser URL changes while the application updates the displayed content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This provides a smoother navigation experience and can reduce unnecessary full-page reloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router helps developers connect URL paths with the appropriate components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Organize Routes Effectively&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A well-organized routing structure makes applications easier to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce application may contain routes for the home page, product listings, individual products, shopping cart, and checkout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should group related routes logically and use consistent naming conventions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For larger applications, route configuration can be separated into dedicated files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Dynamic Route Parameters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dynamic routes allow developers to display different content using a shared component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;} /&amp;gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The product component can retrieve the productId from the URL and use it to request the corresponding product information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach eliminates the need to create separate routes for every product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Nested Routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nested routing is useful when multiple pages share a common layout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider an administrative dashboard with sections for users, reports, analytics, and settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of repeating the dashboard navigation and sidebar in every page, developers can create a parent route with an outlet for displaying nested content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This improves code reuse and creates a consistent user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect Private Routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some application sections should only be accessible to authenticated users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a banking dashboard or personal account page should require authentication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can implement route-level authentication checks to redirect unauthenticated users to a login page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, frontend route protection is only a user-interface convenience. The backend must still verify authentication and authorization for every protected operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Lazy Loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Large applications may contain many pages that users do not need immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router can work with React's lazy loading capabilities to load certain route components only when they are required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This reduces the initial JavaScript bundle and can improve loading performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A suitable fallback interface should be displayed while the component is loading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Navigation Errors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users may enter incorrect URLs, follow outdated links, or attempt to access unavailable pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A professional application should include a meaningful not-found page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also provide suitable error handling for failed data requests and unavailable resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router supports route error handling features that can help organize these experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use URL Search Parameters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Search parameters are useful when a page needs to preserve filters, sorting preferences, or pagination information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a product listing page may use parameters such as category, price range, and page number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keeping this information in the URL allows users to share specific views and revisit them through browser history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Accessibility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigation should be accessible to keyboard users and people using assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use semantic links, descriptive labels, appropriate focus management, and clear page headings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After important navigation changes, the application should communicate the new page context to users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security is a critical part of modern&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt; web development&lt;/a&gt;. ReactJS provides powerful tools for creating interactive interfaces, but developers must follow appropriate security practices to protect application data and prevent vulnerabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A secure React application requires attention to user input, authentication, authorization, API communication, and dependency management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these areas can help developers create reliable applications that protect users and their information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Validate User Input&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;User input should never be considered automatically trustworthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications frequently accept information through registration forms, contact forms, search boxes, and comment sections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should validate data on both the client and server sides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Client-side validation improves the user experience by identifying incorrect information before submission. Server-side validation is essential because attackers can bypass browser-based checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as Zod and Yup can help developers define reusable validation schemas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Cross-Site Scripting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) occurs when malicious scripts are injected into web pages and executed in another user's browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React automatically escapes text values rendered through JSX, which provides protection against many common injection attacks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, developers must be careful when using dangerouslySetInnerHTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If HTML must be displayed from an external source, it should be sanitized using an appropriate and maintained sanitization library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never assume that external HTML is safe simply because it comes from a trusted-looking source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Secure Authentication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication verifies a user's identity before allowing access to an application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React developers often integrate authentication through a backend service or an identity provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A secure authentication system should use appropriate password hashing on the server, secure session management, and protected communication through HTTPS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For browser-based applications, secure, HttpOnly, and appropriately configured SameSite cookies can help protect session credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sensitive authentication tokens should not be casually stored in localStorage because scripts running in the page can access them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apply Authorization Checks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication and authorization are different concepts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication determines who the user is, while authorization determines what the user can access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an employee may be allowed to view a dashboard but not modify administrative settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can hide or display interface elements according to user permissions. However, frontend checks alone are not security controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend must independently verify permissions before processing protected requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect API Communication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications frequently communicate with external APIs to retrieve and submit information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use HTTPS and avoid exposing private API keys or sensitive credentials in frontend code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anything included in a client-side JavaScript bundle should be treated as publicly accessible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Private operations should be performed through a secure backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also implement suitable error handling, request validation, and rate limiting on the server side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect Against CSRF&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) occurs when an attacker tricks a browser into sending an unwanted authenticated request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications using cookie-based authentication should implement appropriate CSRF protections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These may include SameSite cookie settings, anti-CSRF tokens, and server-side origin checks, depending on the architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand the authentication mechanism before choosing the correct protection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep Dependencies Updated&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications often depend on external packages for routing, forms, styling, and state management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outdated dependencies can contain known security vulnerabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should regularly review installed packages, remove unnecessary dependencies, and apply security updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as npm audit can help identify known vulnerabilities in the dependency tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Errors Without Revealing Sensitive Information&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error messages should provide useful information without exposing passwords, authentication tokens, internal server paths, or confidential system details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Error Boundaries can help prevent certain rendering errors from crashing the entire user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, they do not replace backend error handling or comprehensive monitoring.&lt;/p&gt;
